INSERT INTO `chapters` (`id`, `book_id`, `title`, `content`, `sort_order`) VALUES (10,1,'Family and Local Institutions','Unit 1 Family and Local Institutions\n\nNow-a-days, there are very few joint families. Most families are splitting up due\nto the movement of the members of the family to other parts of the country for\njobs, for business or education. These members meet each other on special\noccasions like marriage, birthday parties and other family functions.\nThe members of a family are related to one another in the following ways :\nBy Marriage\n(Husband and wife\nstart a family\nafter marriage.)\nBy Birth\n(Children born to parents\nare related to their\nparents’ families.)\nBy Adoption\n(Sometimes, children are\nadopted into families even\nwhen they are not born to them.\nThen, they become a part\nof the family.)Importance of a Family\nA family lays the foundation of a human being. A family gives us an identity. Our\nparents teach us what is wrong and what is right.\nD Our family is our first school and our parents\nare our first teacher. They teach us good\nvalues.\nD Our family gives us strength to face the\nworld outside.\nD It fulfils all our daily needs.\nD Family members teach and serve one\nanother and also share joys and sorrows of life.\nD It provides love, support and a framework of values to each of its\nmembers.\n6EVS-4\n\nFamily Values\nSome values that we learn from our family are as follow :\nD To be truthful and honest.\nD To respect and obey elders.\nD To help the poor and needy people.\nD To share things with others.\nD To be sincere in all the work that we do.\nD To be polite with all.\nD To keep our house and surroundings clean.\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. A group consisting of parents and their children living together as a unit is\ncalled a :\na) family b) team c) class d) friends\nD A patriarchal family is the one in which a male is the head of the family.\nD A matriarchal family is the one in which a female is the head of the family.\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD A group of people closely related by blood, living together under one\nroof is called a family.\nD A family where a father, a mother and their children live together is\ncalled a nuclear family.\nD A family where more than two generations live together under one\nroof is called a joint family.\nD The members of a family are related to each other by marriage, birth\nor adoption.\nD A family lays the foundation of a human society.\n7EVS-4\n\n2. Joint family is also known as :\na) nuclear family b) extended family\nc) immediate family d) small family\n3. A family gives us :\na) family b) friends\nc) relatives d) an identity\n4. We should talk with everyone :\na) angrily b) politely\nc) sadly d) all of these\nB. Write or :True False\n1. A nuclear family is also known as extended family.\n2. A family is the basic unit of the society.\n3. Husband and wife start a family after marriage.\n4. We should be polite with all.\n5. Family can be big or small.\nC. Fill in the blanks :\n1. A group of people related by marriage, birth and adoption is called a\n____________.\n2. In a joint family, there are more than two live together.___________\n3. Families are spitting up due to the of the members of the family ___________\nto different parts of the .___________\n4. Our family is our first . ____________\n5. A family lays the foundation of a . ____________\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is a nuclear family ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What is a joint family ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What is adoption in families ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n8EVS-4\n\n4. How families are spitting up ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. Distinguish between a patriarchal family and matriarchal family.\n____________________________________________________________________\nYour photograph Your parentsʼ childhood\nphotograph\nDo you see any resemblance ?\nDiscuss and write :\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\nB. Draw your family tree on a chart paper. Paste and label photographs. Use different\ncolours to mark out your ʻimmediateʼ and ʻextendedʼ families.\nWe should love and care our family members. Every person in the family is equally\nimportant. We should always try to make them happy and feel special.\nSuhani forgot to wish her father on his birthday. Her father does not say anything but\nhe is sad. What should Suhani do ?\nDo you know any family where a child is adopted ?\n(Life Skills)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Art Integration)\nTASKS\nA. Paste your picture and your parentsʼ childhood pictures here : (Art Integration)\n9EVS-4\n\nWhat is Occupation ?\nWe need many products and services in our daily life. Like we need grocery, we\nneed milk, we need medicines, we need clothes, we need electronics items, we\nneed cleaning and repairing services and so on. We get these products and\nservices from different people. There are other people who work as teachers,\nlawyers, doctors, engineer or accountant, etc.\nBut why all those people do that work ? They do it to earn money. Jobs that\npeople do to earn money is called their .occupation\nJobs or occupations that need special training and education are called\nprofessions professionals. People who do these jobs are called . Doctors,\nengineers, pilots, teachers and architects are professionals. Some jobs need\nspecial training like that of a mechanic, potter, tailor and carpenter.\nLet us meet a few people at work to take a look at the jobs they do and the\ntools they use :\nTypes of Occupations\nThis is Raghu. He is a . He makes beautiful potter\nclay pots. He uses soft clay on a spinning wheel\nand gives different shapes to his pots. He also\nmakes small diyas which are used during\nDiwali. His wife helps him to paint the pots and\ndiyas.\nPotter\n2\nOccupations and\nProfessions\n• What is Occupation ? • Types of Occupations\n• Gender and Work\n10EVS-4\n\nThis is Nathu. He is a . He stitches clothes. He learnt tailor\nstitching from his mother. He uses a sewing machine to\nstitch the clothes.\nTailor\nBrij is a . He works in a motor mechanic\ngarage. He started his career as a helper.\nOver the years, he learnt the art of repairing\ncars and other vehicles. Now, he is the chief\nmechanic. People love and admire him for\nhis hard work and sincerity.\nMechanic\nPinku is a . He mends shoes, polishes cobbler\nthem and repairs the zips of bags and purses.\nPinku learnt this work from his father in his\nchildhood.\nCobbler\nPilot\nAmit is a . Since his childhood, he pilot\nused to play with aeroplanes. After\ncompleting his higher secondary\neducation, he joined a flying club to learn\nflying. After completing his training, he\ngot a license to fly. Now, he is a working\nas a pilot with Air India and takes\npassengers from one country to another.\n11EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. What is the occupation of your father or mother ?\n2. Do all occupations need specialised education ?\n\nDoctor\nManoj is a . He treats the sick people. He doctor\nuses a stethoscope, thermometer, etc., to\ncheck the patients. He diagnoses various\ndiseases and prescribes medicines. His\nparents were famous doctors and so is he.\nBesides these, there are many other people whose work is very important for\nus :\nD teacher A teaches in the school.\nD farmerA grows food grains.\nD architectAn makes designs of buildings.\nD driver A drives a vehicle.\nGender and Work\nIn the past, women were not encouraged to go to schools and colleges. They\nwere also not allowed to take up a job. However, time has changed now. Now,\nwomen are working in different fields as doctors, teachers, engineers,\nscientists, software programmers, architects, pilots, etc.\nWomen have reached the topmost position also in our country.\nPratibha Patil was the first woman President of our country in 2007 and\nDroupadi Murmu became the second woman President in 2022.\nSania Mirza, Sunita Williams, Kiran Bedi, etc., are some of very famous Indian\nwomen from different fields.\nDroupadi Murmu Sania Mirza Sunita Williams Kiran Bedi\n12EVS-4\nYogita Raghuwanshi is the first\nwoman truck driver of India.\n\nSimilarly, men have also ventured beyond their traditional fields of work. They\nnow work as chefs, fashion designers, hair stylists and make up artists.\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Who works in a hospital ?\na) Policeman b) Lawyer c) Doctor d) Tailor\n2. A potter makes pots for us.____________\na) iron b) copper\nc) earthen d) wooden\n3. A pilot flies a/an :\na) kite b) aeroplane\nc) both a) & b) d) none of these\n4. A barber :\na) mends our shoes b) stitches our clothes\nc) cuts our hair d) treats sick people\nB. Write or :True False\n1. We need special education to become a professional.\n2. We do not need any training and skill to become a potter.\n13EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Different people do different kinds of jobs to earn money.\nD Any work that we do to earn money is called occupation.\nD Jobs that need special training and education are called professions.\nD Some people work as farmers, carpenters, doctors, teachers,\nplumbers, cobblers, green grocers, tailors, nurses, etc.\nD Today, gender plays no role in the selection of an occupation.\nRAPIDTASK\nName any two famous Indian men who have ventured beyond their traditional\nfields of work.\n\n3. A cobbler mends and polishes our shoes.\n4. Only men can reach the top positions in our country.\nC. Whose help will you take :\n1. If your water pipeline is faulty. _______________________________\n2. If someone in intentionally hurts you. _______________________________\n3. If your car needs repairing. _______________________________\n4. If you want to get a dress stitched. _______________________________\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is an occupation ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. Why people work for others ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What service do we get from an electrician ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. What is the job of a lawyer ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. What does a carpenter do ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. There must be many people in your neighbourhood who work as cobbler, potter,\nmechanic, electrician, milkman, washerman, driver, etc. Take an interview of\nany one of them :\nYou can frame such questions.\n1. What is your name ? ___________________________\n2. What work do you do ? ___________________________\n3. From whom did you learn this skill from ? ___________________________\n4. Do you like your work ? ___________________________\n5. How many hours do you work everyday ? ___________________________\n6. Are you able to earn enough ? ___________________________\n(Vocational Skills)\n14EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nDo you think that all the sections of the society including SC, ST, minorities and\nwomen are getting equal opportunity to work ?\nB. Gather information about these professions :\n1. 2. 3.\nEmbroidery Wood Craft Carpet Making\nC. Collect the pictures of special tools that are used in professions given above and\nstick them here :\n• We all should do our work with honesty and sincerity.\n• Do you think every work is important. Whether it is highly paid or lowly paid ?\nWhy / Why not ?\n(Art Integration)\n(Democratic and Human Values)\n(Democratic and Human Values)\n(Experiential Learning)\n15EVS-4\n\nBirds are a beautiful animal. They can fly. Their body is divided into three parts\n– head, trunk, and tail. Birds have legs but no hand. They have wings in place of\ntheir hands.\nThere are more than 9000 species of birds.\nThey are spread over the entire Earth. They are\nof different sizes and many different colours.\nThere whole body is covered with feathers.\nFeathers, beak and claws are their special\nfeatures.\nHead part\nTrunk part\nTail part\nDown Feathers\nThese feathers cover the\nbody and keep it warm.\nThey are small and\nfluffy.\nBody Feathers\nThese feathers cover the\nbody and give a shape\nto it.\nFlight Feathers\nFlight feathers are long\nand flat feathers in the\nwings and tail. They help\nthe bird to fly.\nDifferent Kinds of Feathers\nFeathers\nBirds are the only animals which have feathers. A bird’s body is covered with\nfeathers. Feathers are of different colours in different birds. There are three\nkinds of feathers.\n3\nAmazing Birds\n• Feathers • How do Birds Fly ? • Flying Differently\n• Flightless Birds • Aquatic Birds • Feet and Claws of Birds\n• Birds of Prey • Beaks and Food Habits of Birds\n16EVS-4',1),(11,1,'Birds and Animals','Unit 2 Birds and Animals\n\nHow do Birds Fly ?\nBirds fly with the help of movement of wings. A bird flaps its wings in both\ndirections, upward and the downward, to fly. These movements of wings are\ncalled and respectively.upstroke downstroke\nUpstroke Downstroke\nMovements of Wings of a Flying Bird\nWhen a bird reaches higher in the air, it does not need to flap its wings. The tail\nof a bird acts like the rudder of a boat. It helps the bird to change its direction\nduring the flight.\nFlying Differently\nSome birds like eagle, vulture and\nkite fly at great heights.\nSome birds like sparrow and bulbul fly low.\nSome birds like crow and pigeon fly over\nlong distances.\nSparrow\nKite Eagle\nPigeonCrowHen\nSome birds like hen fly only short\ndistances. They usually walk on legs.\nVulture\n17EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. What is the most special feature of birds ?\n2. What helps the birds in flying ?\n\nFlightless Birds\nSome birds like kiwi, ostrich, penguin do not fly at all because their bodies are\ntoo heavy. These birds are called . They move on with the help of flightless birds\ntheir legs.\nKiwi Penguin Ostrich\nAquatic Birds\nAquatic birds live in water. They spend most of their time in water, either\nfloating on the surface, diving to catch fish, swimming gracefully or simply\nwading through the water. These birds have oil glands which do not allow their\nfeathers to get wet or spoiled by water. These birds have webbed toes which\nhelp them to swim in water. Ducks, swans, cranes, gulls and pelicans are\ncommon water birds.\nFeet and Claws of Birds\nDifferent birds have different types of feet and claws.\nThe claws of a bird help the bird in perching, walking, climbing and swimming.\nThey also help in catching, holding and eating the food. Claws protect the birds\nfrom their enemies.\nDuck Swan PelicanCrane\n18EVS-4\n\nLet us know about some common types of feet and claws of birds :\nScratching Birds\nScratching birds like hens and quails have strong feet\nwith three toes in front and one short toe at the back.\nEach of these toes has a sharp horny claw. These birds\nscratch the ground to bring out the buried food, seeds\nand insects from the ground. Scratching birds walk and\nrun on the ground.\nPerching Birds\nPerching birds like crow and sparrow have four toes in\ntheir feet— three in front and one at the back. These toes\nhelp such birds in perching, that is to hold the branch of\na tree very firmly even when the bird is asleep.\nPerching birds may also hop on the ground.\nBirds of Prey\nBirds of prey like eagle, vulture, hawk and owl have\nvery strong feet with sharp claws called to catch talons\nand hold their prey firmly. These birds may carry their\nprey to long distances.\nSwimming Birds\nSwimming birds like duck and swan have webbed feet.\nTheir feet have special webs of skin between toes. A\nwebbed foot is like a paddle. Webbed toes help birds to\npush back water while swimming.\nClimbing Birds\nClimbing birds like parrots and woodpeckers have four\ntoes—two pointing upwards and two downwards.\nThese toes help the bird to cling to the bark of trees\nand to climb up.\nA Scratching Bird\nand its Toes\nA Perching Bird\nand its Toes\nA Climbing Bird\nand its Toes\nA Bird of Prey\nand its Toes\nA Swimming Bird\nand its Toes\n19EVS-4\n\nA Wading Bird\nand its Toes\nPeacock\nShort and Broad Beak\nof a Swallow\nWading Birds\nWading birds like crane and paddy birds have thin and\nlong feet with wide spreading toes. These toes keep the\nfeet safe from sinking in the soft mud.\nBeaks and Food Habits of Birds\nBirds eat grains, seed, fruits and flesh. They use their\nbeak to catch and hold food. Birds do not have hands\nand teeth. Their beak tear, bite, chisel and crush the\nfood. Different birds have different types of beak. Its\nshape and size depend on their food habits.\nSome birds used their beaks to fight with the enemy or catch the prey or use it\nto clean the feathers. According to their food habits, beaks are of different types.\nShort and Broad Beak\nBirds like swallows have short and broad beaks which are\nsticky inside. These birds move round and round in the air\nkeeping their beaks open. During flight the small flying\ninsects enter the beak and get stuck inside the mouth.\nShort, Hard and Horny Beak\nBirds like sparrows, pigeons and\npeacocks eat nuts, grains and seeds.\nThey have short, hard, pointed and\nhorny beaks to break the food into\nsmall pieces.\nSparrow Pigeon\nShort, Hard and Horny Beaks\n20EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Can all birds fly ? Name any\nthree which cannot fly.\n2. Can all birds swim ? Name\nany three which can swim.\nFlamingos stand on both legs while they feed.\nWhen they rest, they stand on one leg\nbecause this helps them keep their balance\nbetter.\n\nCurved Beak\nA parrot has a curved beak. It helps it to crack nuts and\nhard fruits. The curved beak also helps it to climb.\nBroad and Flat Beak\nA duck has broad and flat beak. It has tiny holes called\nshovels strainers at the sides and fine holes called all\nalong the edges.\nSuch a beak helps the duck to dig the mud near the\npond, to pick up insects, worms and water plants.\nWater and mud flow out from the strainers and shovels\nleaving insects, worms and water plants in the mouth.\nStrong, Sharp and Hook-shaped Beak\nPreying birds like eagle, vulture, hawk, kite and owl have strong, sharp and hook-\nshaped beaks. This type of beak helps them in tearing the flesh into small pieces.\nCurved Beak of Parrot\nBroad and Flat\nBeak of Duck\nEagle Vulture Kite Owl Hawk\nStrong, Sharp and Hook-shaped Beaks\nBroad, Long and Pointed Beak\nBirds like stork and kingfisher have broad, long\nand pointed beaks. They help them to catch fish\nfrom the water.\nStrong and Heavy Beak\nA woodpecker has a strong and heavy beak. This type of\nbeak is also called . This beak helps it to chisel-shaped beak\npick up food and to make holes in the trunks of the trees.\nStrong and Heavy Beak\nof Woodpecker\nBroad, Long and Pointed\nBeak of Kingfisher\n21EVS-4\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Birds have in place of their hands.____________\na) feathers b) wings\nc) tail d) beak\n2. This bird flies at great heights.\na) Eagle b) Pigeon\nc) Sunbird d) Parrot\n3. It is a flightless bird.\na) Penguin b) Vulture\nc) Parrot d) Eagle\nLong, Thin and Pointed Beak\nA sunbird has a long, thin and pointed beak. It helps the\nbird to suck juice from the fruits and flowers.\nLong and Slender Beak\nA hoopoe has a long and slender beak.\nIt helps it to pull out insects from holes in the ground.Long and Slender\nBeak of Hoopoe\nLong, Thin and Pointed\nBeak of Sunbird\n22EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD There are more than 9000 species of birds in the world.\nD Birds are beautiful animals.\nD Feathers are of different colours in different birds.\nD A bird flaps its wings in both the upward and the downward direction\nto fly.\nD The tail of a bird acts like the rudder of a boat.\nD The claws of a bird help the bird in perching, walking, climbing and\nswimming.\nD Chisel-shaped beak helps to pick up food and to make holes in the\ntrunks of the trees.\n\n4. Birds fly with the help of their .____________\na) feathers b) wings\nc) claws d) none of these\nB. State or :True False\n1. Tail keeps the bird’s body warm.\n2. The tail of a bird acts like the rudder of a boat.\n3. Crow and pigeon fly at great heights.\n4. Eagle and vulture fly only for short distances.\nC. Fill in the blanks :\n1. The feathers cover a bird’s body.______________\n2. Birds body is made up of three parts head, trunk and ._______________\n3. Kiwi is a bird._____________\n4. Aquatic birds have toes to swim in water._____________\nD. Match the following :\n1. Ostrich a) Scratching bird\n2. Sparrow b) Bird of prey\n3. Hen c) Perching bird\n4. Crane d) Flightless bird\n5. Hawk e) Wading bird\nE. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is the most important and unique feature of birds ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What are talons ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. Name the three kinds of feathers. How do they help a bird ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. What are the three sections of the birds’ body ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. Why do different birds have different types of beaks ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n23EVS-4\n\nDo you think a woodpecker will be able to survive if it has thin and a pointed beak ?\nGive reason for your answer.\nDo you know what kinds of birds live in your neighborhood ? Have you ever taken a\nclose look at the birds and observe their behaviour ? In this activity, you will build a\nbird feeder to attract birds, so you can study them :\nMaterials\nD Plastic milk/juice container with cap D Scissors or craft knife\nD Wooden dowel, twig or wooden coffee stirrer\nD Bird seeds D Mini cup(s) D Water\nD Tape D Optional : paper plate\nD Possible decorations : tree bark, shells, stones,\nstickers, etc.\nD Optional : paintbrushes, when using paint\nD Glue or glue gun D Pen or pencil\nFollow the steps :\n1. With the help of an adult, cut a hole into the flat side of the container. It\nshould be 1 inch above the bottom of the container and three inches in\ndiameter. You can have two such holes on two different sides.\n2. Use tape to cover the sharp edges of the opening so the birds donʼt get\nharmed when coming into your bird feeder.\n3. Cut a small hole below the opening and insert a wooden dowel or stick to\nbuild a perch for the birds. Secure the stick in place with glue.\n4. Glue one or more mini cups onto the floor of your bird feeder or onto the\ninside walls.\n5. Prepare a place for your bird food. You can either put a paper plate into your\nbird feeder or plan to put the seeds directly in the container.\n6. Decorate the feeder with different colours.\n7. Fill the bird feeder with seeds and add water in the mini cup.\n8. Hang the bird feeder on tree or set it on a flat surface at a higher level.\nObserve the birds that come to your bird feeder.\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Experiential Learning)\n24EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nMost of the animals are social in nature. They live and more around in groups to\nprotect each other from harsh weather, to defend themselves against enemies\nand to look for food. They help each other in bringing up their young ones.\nThey feel safe when they are together.\nGroups of Animals\nThe oldest or the strongest member of the group becomes the group leader.\nThe leader warns the other members about danger. Cows, zebras, horses,\nelephants, buffaloes and deer live in groups. Their group is called a .herd\nA group of chimpanzees, gorillas or monkeys is called a .band\nA group of lions is called a . A pride consists of two or more adult lions, pride\nseveral lionesses and their cubs.\nThe dog family that includes wolves, foxes, jackals and coyotes move in .packs\nA Pride of Lions A Band of Monkeys A Herd of Elephants\n4\nAnimal\'s World\n• Groups of Animals • Friendly Animals\n• Shy Animals • Give-and-Take Relationship\n• Ears of Animals • Hair and Fur\n25EVS-4\nThe group of caterpillars is called an army of caterpillars while the group of elks is\ncalled a gang.\n\nFriendly Animals\nInsects such as ants, honeybees and termites live together in groups called\ncolonies. The colony of a honeybees has one queen bee, about a hundred\ndrones (males) and more than thousand worker bees.\nA Colony of AntsA Pack of Wolves\nFriendly Animals\nSome animals are friendly and allow us to go near them or feed them. If we give\nthem food, they will immediately come forward to eat it. Cats, cows and dogs are\ngenerally friendly by nature. Humans have tamed many friendly animals. Most of\nthe farm animals like cows, buffaloes, goats, hens and ducks are also friendly\nwith us.\nDolphins are friendly sea creatures. They live in\ngroups. They learn to play tricks easily and\nentertain people. Shows of dolphins performing\ntricks are very popular in abroad. A Dolphin\nA band of chimpanzees can have from 15 to 80 members.\n26EVS-4\n\nBabies of animals enjoy playing. Kittens love to have a friendly fight.\nSometimes, they roll over or pull each other.\nShy Animals\nThere are many animals who run away when\nsomeone approaches them. They are scared of\npeople. These animals are not familiar with\nhuman beings and may sometimes harm them,\ntoo. Rabbits, lizards, squirrels, rats, deer and\nmost of the birds and insects are shy animals.\nTortoise and snails go into their shells if they\nfeel someone is going to harm them. Shy Animals\nGive-and-Take Relationship\nSome species of animals share special relationship with animals of other\nspecies. They help each other.\nD cattle egretsWhite birds called live near large animals such as cow,\nbuffaloes, elephants and deer. They do so because they eat the insects\nthat associate with the cattle. Egrets often perch on the backs of large\nanimals. If the birds sense any danger, they fly away. This is a warning sign\nfor the deer or cattle.\nD Oxpeckers are small birds that ride on large animals such as oxen, rhinos\nand giraffes and peck the insects off their bodies.\nD remoraA is a small fish which sticks to a shark. It eats small creatures that\nlive on the shark’s body.\n27EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Name three shy animals you find around you :\na) _______________ b) _______________ c) _______________\n2. Name three friendly animals :\na) _______________ b) _______________ c) _______________\n\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Why do animals make group ?\n2. Do all animals live in groups ?\nD Egyptian Plover, a bird that feeds on bits of decaying meat stuck in the\nteeth of crocodile. The plover gets its food and the crocodile gets its teeth\ncleaned.\nD Birds and monkeys make noise to alert other animals, if lions, tigers and\nleopards are coming near.\nD Penguins huddle together to keep themselves and their babies warm.\nCattle Egret A Crocodile and Egyptian\nPlover\nOxpeckers and giraffe\nRemora Fish and Shark\nEars of Animals\nAll animals use their ears to hear sound. Some animals\nhave small ears while some animals have long ears.\nAmongst the animals that have ears, there are some\nanimals with ears that are visible, called .external ears\nMammals, including human beings, have external ears,\none on each side of the head.\nAn elephant has big external ears. It uses them to hear\nand to fan itself.\nDonkeys, rabbits and giraffes also have big ears.\n28EVS-4\n\nWe cannot see the ears of some animals. These\nanimals have . Birds have tiny holes internal ears\non both sides of their head. These are covered\nwith feathers. Snakes and lizards do not have ears.\nThey feel the vibrations on the ground and know\nabout the presence of other animals.\nHair and Fur\nSome animals have hair on their body. Some animals have long hair on their\nskin and some animals have small. Animals like yaks, musk oxen and bears that\nlive in very cold areas have long hair which keep them warm. Animals like cows\nand buffaloes have short hair.\nYakCow Buffalo\nYou will observe that the animals which have external ears also have body hair.\nSuch animals are called .mammals\nAnimals like porcupine and hedgehog have needle like hair on their body. It\nhelps them to protect themselves from their enemies.\nThe different designs on an animals’ skin are due to the presence of different\ncoloured hair.\nA leopard has spots on its body. Tigers have striped coat.\n29EVS-4\nD Although hippopotamus is a big animal, it has small ears.\nD It seems that the snake is dancing to the tune of the snake charmer. But\nactually, it just follows the movement of snake charmer’s bean!\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. A group of elephants is called :\na) society b) locality\nc) colony d) herd\n2. Animals like dogs, cats, cows and goats are animals.______________\na) aggressive b) friendly\nc) unfriendly d) shy\n3. Which of these sticks to a shark ?\na) Remora b) Oxpecker\nc) Egyptian Plover d) Cattle Egrets\nTigerLeopardHedgehogPorcupinePorcupine\n30EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Animals that live in groups have a leader who guides the other group\nmembers.\nD Some animals are shy and some are friendly.\nD Many animals help each other.\nD Animals like elephants, donkeys, rabbits and giraffes have external\nears. Birds have internal ears.\nD Mammals are animals that have external ears and body hair.\nD Some animals do not have ears. They feel the vibrations on the ground\nand know the presence of other animals.\n\n4. An animal that lives in a colony is :\na) ant b) dolphin\nc) cow d) elephant\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. is a small fish that sticks to shark for its food.________________\n2. are friendly sea creatures.________________\n3. An has big external ears.________________\n4. Birds have tiny on both sides of their head.________________\nC. Give reasons for the following :\n1. Tortoise go into their shell when they fell danger near them.\n2. Animals live in groups.\n3. Some animals have hair on their body.\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. How can you say that animals are social in nature ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. Who becomes the leader of the group ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What are shy animals ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. How does the egret riding on a buffalo help the buffalo ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. What are external ears ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Adopt a street dog from your neighbourhood. Give food and water to it and see\nhow it becomes friendly and faithful towards you.\nB. Arctic Animalsʼ Camouflage\nThis activity will help you get to know the arctic animals and learn about how they\ndepend on seasonal comouflage to survive. Students will pick one arctic animal\nand research facts about it.\n(Democratic and Human Values)\n(Scientific Temper)\n31EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nMost animals understand the safety and security that comes with unity. But there few\nanimals that prefer to live alone. Find out from the Internet.\nDivide the class in 4-5 group, assign an animal from the given list :\nD Snowshoe Hare D Peary Caribou\nD Arctic Lemming D Ptarmigan\nD Arctic Fox D Polar Bear\nD Arctic Wolf D Harp Seal\nD Snowy Owl D Beluga Whale\nC. Look at the picture below and write the names of the animals in the relevent\ncolumn :\nIt is rainy season and a stray dog has given birth to puppies, in your colony. They do\nnot have any home to live in, would you build a home for them ? (Democratic and Human Values)\n(Digital Resources)\nAnimals with Fur Animals with Feathers\n(Conceptual Learning)\n32EVS-4\n\nTrees and plants are seen in our home, schools, parks, roadsides and in\nmaidans. They are found in different shapes and sizes. They all have different\ntypes of leaves, flowers and fruits.\nTrees and plants are very important for our environment. They add beauty to\nour surroundings. They give us fiber, medicine flowers, fruits and vegetables.\nWe make houses with the help of the wood. We make cloth with the help of the\ncotton. Wood and cotton come from plants. They purify our air by absorbing\ncarbondioxide and generating oxygen. This oxygen is essential to life. They also\nprovide shade and shelter to many animals and birds.\nParts of Plants\nThe structure of a plant is as interesting\nas the human body.\nA plant can be divided into two parts—\nthe shoot system and the root system.\nThe Shoot System\nThe of a plant is that part of shoot system\nthe plant that grows above the ground.\nIt consists of the following parts of the\nplant :\nD D D Stem Flower Leaf\nD D Fruit Bud\nFruit\nFlower\nLeaf\nRoot\nStem\nBud\nShoot SystemRoot System\nParts of Plant\n33EVS-1\n5\nSystem of Plants\n• Parts of Plants • The Shoot System • The Root System\n• Trees Around Us • Caring for Plants',2),(12,1,'Plants and Geography','Unit 3 Plants and Geography\n\nLet us read about the shoot system of a plant in detail :\nStem\nThe part of a plant just above the ground is called the . The stem of a tree stem\nis the strongest part of the tree and is known as .trunk\nThe stem performs many important functions :\nD It supports all the parts of the plant.\nD It carries water and minerals from the roots to the leaves.\nD It carries prepared food from the leaves to other parts of the plant.\nD It stores food in some plants. Stems of these plants grow below the\nground. These stems are called .underground modified stems\nUnderground Modified Stems\nPotato\nScale\nleaf\nBud\nEye\nGinger\nScale leaf\nBud\nLeaf\nLeaves are thin, flat and green part of a plant.\nWe can identify a plant by its leaves because\nthe leaves of different plants are different.\nLeaves are known as the Kitchen of the Plant\nbecause the food is made here. A leaf has\ngreen pigment called . The leaves chlorophyll\nmake food in the presence of sunlight, water, air and minerals. This process is\ncalled .photosynthesis\nOxygen\nCarbon\ndioxide\nWater\nFood travels to other parts\nSun\nPhotosynthesis\n34EVS-1\nSome shallowly rooted trees found in the rainforests have roots growing on all the\nsides. Such roots are called buttress roots. They prevent the trees from falling and\ngather more nutrients for the tree.\n\nBud\nBud is a baby flower. It grows into a beautiful flower.\nBud\nFlower\nFlower is the most beautiful part of a plant. Some flowers\nhave sweet smell.\nRose\nFruit\nFruit is the delicious part of a plant. Some fruits\nare eaten raw, while some fruits called\nvegetables are eaten after cooking.\nFruits and Vegetables\nThe Root System\nThe is usually found underground. In some plants, roots also grow root system\nabove the ground. They keep the plants erect at one place. They absorb water\nand minerals from the soil. They also check soil erosion.\nThere are four different types of roots :\nTap Root\nLike carrot and radish, tap roots grow long\nand thick into the soil. Tiny hair-like roots\ngrow out of the main root to absorb water\nand minerals.\nFibrous Root\nLike grass and rice the primary root dies at\nan early stage and numerous roots grow\nfrom the lower part of the stem. These roots\nappear as a bunch of fibres as many small\nroots grow sideways.\nTap Root\nFibrous Root\n35EVS-1\n\nStorage Root\nThe vegetables like carrot, turnip, tapioca, etc., are storage roots. They store\nfood prepared by the leaves and are rich sources of energy.\nRadish Turnip Carrot Sweet potato\nStorage Roots\nAerial Roots\nThese roots grow out of the branches of huge\ntrees. They grow towards the soil and support the\ntree. For example, banyan tree.\nAerial roots of Banyan Tree\nFlowering trees, like cherry and\nmuskogee are pleasant to look at.\nCherry Muskogee\nWe also see common trees like\neucalyptus and banyan around us.\nEucalyptus Banyan\nIn an orchard, we see fruit trees\nlike apple and pineapple.\nApple Pineapple\nTrees Around Us\nThere are different kinds of trees surrounding us.\n36EVS-1\nBamboo is the fastest growing\nplant. One of its species can\ngrow up to 35 inches per day.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Have you ever grown a plant ?\n2. How should we take care of the\nplants ?\n3. Have you ever taken any vegetable\nor fruit directly from a farm ?\n4. Why some plants are kept inside the\nhouse ?\n\nIn the cold hilly areas,\nwe find trees like fir and pine.\nTrees like teak and shisham\nprovide us with wood.\nFir Pine Teak Shisham\nDo not cut trees\nCaring for Plants\nWe should take proper care of plants as they\nare very useful to us. We should not cut down\ntrees. We should not allow anyone to cut down\na tree. Cutting trees will lead to serious\nproblems like pollution, drought and flooding.\nThere are many Government agencies that\ntake care of trees in their area of work. These\nagencies employ gardeners to take care of trees. They plant trees along road\nsides and other suitable places. They water them regularly.\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Plants and trees are very important for our .______________\na) roads b) houses c) environment d) none of these\n37EVS-1\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Plants are very important for our environment.\nD A plant has mainly two parts—the shoot system and the root system.\nD The shoot system is the part of a plant which is above the ground.\nD The root system is the part of a plant which is under the soil.\nD Leaf is known as the ‘Kitchen of the Plant’.\nD We should not cut down trees.\n\n2. is the kitchen of a plant.________\na) Root b) Stem\nc) Leaf d) Bud\n3. The part of the plant just above the ground is called ._________\na) stem b) leaf\nc) flower d) fruit\n4. is not a part of the shoot system.________\na) Root b) Bud\nc) Flower d) stem\nB. Name two plants which :\n1. Give us wood for furniture _____________ ____________\n2. Have tap roots _____________ ____________\n_____________ ____________3. Have fibrous roots\n_____________ ____________4. Store food in their leaves\nC. Match the following :\n1. The strongest part of a tree a) food maker of the plant\n2. Leaf b) rice\n3. Tap root c) banyan tree\n4. Fibrous root d) stem\n5. Aerial root e) carrot\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. How plants are important for us and our environment ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What does the shoot system consist of ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What is root system ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. Write the main functions of leaves.\n____________________________________________________________________\n38EVS-1\n\nA. Collect pictures of various leaves, fruits, vegetables, flowers and buds. Paste them\nand name them in your scrapbook.\nB. Name the following :\n1. Part of the plant that grows above ground ___________________\n2. Roots that grow out of branches of large tree ___________________\n3. Most beautiful part of a plant ___________________\n4. Process of making food by the plants ___________________\n5. Kitchen of the plant ___________________\nC. Hydroponics\nPlants can grow without soil, too, using a process called hydroponics. In this\nactivity, you will transform a two liter water bottle into a mini-garden that needs\nonly light, air, and nutrient-rich water to grow. In this activity, we will try to learn\nhydroponics—the process of growing plants in nutrient-rich water.\nNutrients are not found in pure water. We need to add them.\n1. Measure 1 quarter filtered, or purified water into a container. Avoid\nunfiltered tap water. Mix that into the water in your second bottle or\ncontainer as per the instruction given on the bottle.\n2. Place three seeds in the coconut coir just above the knot in the wick.\n3. Cover the seeds with the coir.\n4. Pour the prepared water over the growing medium. It will seep into the\nreservoir. Keep pouring until the reservoir is about 1/2 to 2/3 full. The water\nshould never reach the spout of the bottle. Make sure that the area around\nthe seeds or tiny plant gets wet as you pour the water.\n5. Once the seed is sprouted, place the container near a window as now the\nplant needs air and sunlight too.\n6. Check daily to make sure the area around the seed or tiny plant is moist. Add\na little water if the level goes down. As the plant grows, its roots will extend\ndeeper and get better access to the water and nutrients sucked up by the\nwick.\nHow do honeybees make honey ? What do you mean by apiculture ?\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Scientific Temper)\n(Art Integration)\n(Critical Thinking)\n39EVS-1\nTASKS\n\nPlants are of two types—flowering and non-flowering. Some flowering plants\nare rose, jasmine, sunflower, lotus, tulip and daisy.\nFlowers are beautiful and the most attractive parts of a plant. There are\ndifferent kinds of flowers. They are of different shapes, sizes and colours.\nFlowers grow from buds. When buds open, petals spread out to form flowers.\nParts of a Flower\nPetal\nStigma\nStyleAnther\nFilament\nOvule Ovary\nSepal\nParts of a Flower\nA flower has different parts as shown in the picture.\nPlants reproduce through flowers. The colourful\npetals attract insects like butterflies, etc. They\ncollect pollen from the stamen as food. Then they\ntransfer this pollen to the pistil of another flower to\nwhich they go. This process is called . It pollination\nhelps to produce seeds.\nFunctions of a Flower\nSeeds are found in fruits. Flowers wither and change into fruits. Fruits have\nseeds inside them. When conditions are favourable, seeds germinate and grow\ninto new plants.\nNew plant grows from a seed\nFlower Fruit Seed New Plant\n6\nValley of Flowers\n40EVS-4\n• Parts of a Flower • Functions of Flowers\n• Flowering and Non-flowering Plants • Uses of Flowers\n• How do we get Flowers ?\n\nFlowering Plants\nDifferent types of plants bear flowers in\ndifferent seasons. There are different\nflowers in different seasons. There are\nsome flowering plants which bear flowers\nthroughout the year.\nSunflower Tulip\nMarigold Rose\nFlowering Plants in Spring Flowering Plants in Autumn\nJasmine Orchid\nDahlia Sweet pea\nFlowering Plants in SummerFlowering Plants in Winter\nNon-flowering Plants\nAll some plants do not bear flowers. Such plants are called . non-flowering plants\nMoss, mushroom, fern, mould, etc., are some examples of non-flowering plants.\nMushroomMoss Fern\nUses of Flowers\nA flower sometimes known as a . Flowers are used in various bloom blossomor\nways.\nTitan arum is the world’s tallest\nflower, about 2-5 metre tall. It\ngrows in the forests of Sumatra.\n41EVS-4\n\nLet us read about some uses of flowers :\nD Flowers are used for worship.\nD Flowers are used to make perfume.\nD Women wear flowers in their hair as gajra.\nD Some flowers are used as herbal medicines.\nD Flowers are used to decorate houses, offices, etc.\nD Gulkand is made from rose flower.\nD Honeybees collect nectar from flowers to make honey.\nD Garlands of flowers are used in various ceremonies.\nHow do we get Flowers ?\nMany people plant flowers around their houses. We have many gardens in our\ncities which are maintained by the governments.\nFlowers are also sold in markets. People who sell flowers are called .florists\nThese flowers are brought from different cities. Some flowers are imported\nfrom other countries as well. Florists usually sell bouquets.\nGajra\nA Flower ShopA Flower Garden A Florist\nFlowers make our world a beautiful place. They bring joy and happiness to our\nlives. Do not pluck flowers. They look nice in the garden.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Which is the most beautiful part of the plant ?\n2. What are two different types of plants ?\n42EVS-4\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Rose, jasmine, lotus are examples of plants.______________\na) flowering b) non-flowering\nc) both a) & b) d) none of these\n2. Plants reproduce through .______________\na) stem b) flowers\nc) leaves d) roots\n3. is an example of non-flowering plant.______________\na) Mushroom b) Mango\nc) Rose d) Mint\nB. Write or :True False\n1. Seeds are found in fruits.\n2. Flowers grow from the fruits.\n3. All plants bear flowers.\n4. The colourful petals attract insects like butterflies, etc.\nC. Fill in the blanks :\n1. are found inside the fruits.___________\n2. Orchid bears flowers in season. ___________\n3. germinate and grow into new plants.___________\n4. Some flowers are used as herbal .______________\nD. Write the seasons of the following plants :\n1. Rose : _____________________\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD A flower has different parts.\nD A plant can be a flowering plant or a non-flowering plant.\nD Flowers grow from buds.\nD Flowers are the reproductive part of a plant.\nD Insects collect pollen from the stamen and transfer it to the pistil.\n43EVS-4\n\n2. Tulip : _____________________\n3. Zinnia : _____________________\n4. Sunflower : _____________________\nE. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is the importance of flowers in our life ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What is pollination ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What are the different parts of a flower ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Collect pictures of your favourite flowers and paste them in your scrapbook.\nB. Visit a garden and make a list of flowers that you see there. Write their names and\nthe season when they grow in your scrapbook.\nC. Find out the names of flowers that are used for :\n1. Oils : ____________________ ____________________\n2. Perfumes : ____________________ ____________________\n3. Essence : ____________________ ____________________\n4. Face Pack : ____________________ ____________________\nD. Label the parts of a flower in the given boxes :\nHow many species of flowering plants are there in India ?\n(Art Integration)\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Conceptual Learning)\n(Knowledge of India)\nTASKS\n44EVS-4\n\nAll work and no-play makes our mind and body dull.\nGames are fun. Games keep us active and healthy. Games make our mind\nsharp. Games help us learning teamwork.\nGames Played At School\nChildren enjoy playing games in the classroom. Classroom games are generally\nrelated to learning. Crossword, dumb charades, quiz, etc., are\nsome common games that children play in the classroom.\nThese games are either played in\nsmall groups or they involve the\nwhole class.\nDuring the games period, children\nplay different outdoor games like\ncricket, badminton, basketball,\nfootball and kho-kho. They need\ntraining to play these games.\n45EVS-4\n7\nRecreation\n• Games Played at School • Play Fair Games\n• Games Played at Home • Visiting Fair • Visiting Circus\nRAPIDTASK\n1. What games do you play at your school ?\n2. What are the indoor games that you play with your family members ?\nThe first sports as we know it today was most likely wrestling, which originated in\nGreece.',3),(13,1,'Culture and Traditions','Unit 4 Culture and Traditions\n\nExperts of the games train children how to play these games. The school\nprovides all the things needed for these games. The children return these\nplaythings to the sports teacher when the games period is over.\nPlay Fair Games\nChildren get into arguments and fight while\nplaying. If such a thing happens, you should\ntake the help of a teacher or an adult to\nsettle the issue. The best way of solving\nquarrels is to follow the rules of the games\nstrictly. Even grown ups get into arguments\nwhile playing. Each sport has an umpire or a\nreferee to see that the game is played fairly\nand according to the rules. A good sport person has sportsman’s spirit which\nhelps him/her to overcome all obstacles.\nGames Played at Home\nAt home, children play both indoor and outdoor games. They play with their\nfamily members, friends and neighbours.\nLudo, chess, carrom, etc., are some indoor games played by the children.\nNow-a-days, children love to play games on\ncomputers and mobiles. Children play outdoor\ngames in parks and playgrounds in the\nevening. They play cricket, football,\nhide and seek and many other games in\nthe park.\nCricket\nKite Flying\nKite Flying\nIn olden days, people use to recreate\nthemselves with sports, like kite flying,\nspinning tops, playing marbles and\ngames like kabaddi and kho-kho.\n46EVS-4\n\nSpinning Tops\nTop lattu or is a pear-shaped toy usually made of wood. A\ntop gets into motion by pulling a string that has been\nwrapped around it. Children enjoy seeing the spinning top.\nVisiting Fair\nFairs are one of the most enjoyable means of recreation\nand enjoyment.\nBig fairs are organized on the occasion of festivals like\nDussehra, Diwali, Christmas, New Year, etc.\nThere are many stalls at a fair. People enjoy eating different kinds of food here.\nChildren love to play games at different stalls. There are many games where\nthey win prizes, too. There are many swings, slides and other rides for children\nto enjoy in the fair. It is a good means of recreation.\nVisiting Circus\nCircus is a group of performers who travel from one place to another to\nentertain people. Acrobats, jugglers, trapeze artists, tightrope walkers and\nclowns amuse the audience by their amazing acts. Many animals are trained to\nentertain the people.\nClowns or jokers dress up in funny costumes and do make-up to entertain the\naudience with their humorous acts.\nCircus\n47EVS-4\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. All works and no play makes us .______________\na) activity b) smart c) healthy d) dull\n2. Big fairs are organised on the occasions of .______________\na) exams b) festivals\nc) pandemic d) none of these\n3. The shape of a top is :\na) apple like b) pear like c) diamond like d) none of these\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. We play games to our mind.________________\n2. and are some common classroom games.________________ ______________\n3. Each sport has its own set of and .________________ ________________\n4. Children play both and games.________________ ________________\n5. There are many games available on and also._____________ _____________\n48EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Have you ever visited a circus ? How was your experience ?\n2. Which National festival is associated with Kite flying ?\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD We have fun when we do things to enjoy. This is called recreation.\nD Children enjoy playing different games.\nD We must follow the rules of the games.\nD A good sportsperson has sportsman’s spirit.\nD Children love to play with their neighbourhood friends.\nD In olden days, people used to recreate themselves with sports like kite\nflying, spinning tops, playing marbles and games like Kabaddi and\nkho-kho, etc.\nD Children have fun at fairs and at a circus.\n\nC. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is recreation ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. Why should we play games ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. Which games are better indoor or outdoor ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. Why should we play a game according to its rules ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. What is a fair ? Write two lines on any fair that you have visited.\n____________________________________________________________________\nList the activities in appropriate columns :\nCamping Dancing Net Surfing Enjoying at Beach\nWatching TV Fishing Listening to Music Reading Book\nShopping Flying Kite\nIndoor Leisure Activities Outdoor Leisure Activities\n________________________________ ________________________________\n________________________________ ________________________________\n________________________________ ________________________________\n________________________________ ________________________________\n________________________________ ________________________________\nTalk to your friend.\n• What do you like to do in your free time ?\n• How often do you do it ?\n• With whom do you share that recreational activity ?\n• Name an activity which you donʼt like to do.\nWhy the circuses are losing their popularity day by day ?\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Communication Skills)\n(Critical Thinking)\n49EVS-4\nTASKS\n\n50EVS-4\n8 Safety and\nFirst-Aid\n• Safety • First-Aid\nSafety\nWhen we work, study or play, we move or handle many things. Thus, we can get\nharmed due to the various types of movements.\nFollowing are some safety rules that should be followed by all to avoid the\naccidents :\nDo not disturb the\nbus driver.\nDo not jump on\nthe desks.\nAlways stand in a queue at\nthe bus stop.\nUse zebra crossing while\ncrossing the road.\nDo not get in or out of a\nmoving bus.\nDo not lean out of the\nmoving bus.',4),(14,1,'Caring for self and others','Unit 5 Caring for self and others\n\nAvoid using blades to\nsharpen pencils.\nDo not keep toys on\nthe floor.\nKeep your things at the\nright place.\n51EVS-4\nD o not go alone for\nswimming. D o not touch electric wires. D o not run on the stairs.\nFirst-aid\nAn immediate medical aid before an injured person is shifted to the hospital for\naccurate and treatment is called . Timely help can save the diagnosis first-aid\nlife of the injured person. First-aid is given for minor , nose bleeding wounds\nand minor burns. It is not an alternative of complete medical treatment. Any\nperson can give first-aid but he must get basic training to do so.\nFollowing is a list of first-aid given in different kinds of injuries :\nSprain\nThe twisting and swelling up of joints, such as the\nankle or wrist results in sprain. The tissues around\nthe twisted joint are damaged and causes pain.\nv The sprained part should not be moved\nbefore it is healed.\n\n52EVS-4\nv For first-aid, a cold ice-pack should be used on the affected area.\nv Apply the ointment on the sprained joint two or three times a day.\nv Wrap the elastic bandage around the sprained joint after applying the\nointment.\nFracture\nA crack or break in bone is called . The fracture\nfractured bone causes a lot of pain. The affected area\nmay swell up and it will not be able to be moved.\nFollowing are first-aid steps in case of fracture :\nv Do not allow the person to move.\nv Tie a or a to give support to the splin slingt\nbroken bone.\nThe fractured arm can be supported by a . It sling\ngives support to the arm and prevents its movement.\nSling\nSplint\nBurns\nBurns are caused due to heat, chemicals etc.\nv In case of minor burns, the affected skin develops a\nreddish colour.\nv Wash the affected area with cold water.\nv After that, apply burnol and place clean cloth on it to prevent any infection.\nv In case of severe burns, are formed. The blisters must never be blisters\npricked with a pin or any other sharp object. It may lead to infection.\nv Instead, a wet cloth piece dipped in a solution of baking soda can be\napplied on the burnt area.\nv In case, if clothes of a person catches fire roll up the person in a blanket.\nv Take the victim to the doctor immediately after the first-aid is administered.\nv If it is a chemical burn, the burnt area must be washed with lots of water to\nrinse away the chemical and then the victim must be taken to the doctor\nimmediately.\n\nMinor Cuts And Wounds\nThe wound should be cleaned with soap and water and\nthen with cotton soaked in dettol or savlon. If there is\nbleeding from the wound, ice will stop or minimize the\nflow of blood. Apply an antiseptic ointment, cover with\ngauze and tie a bandage.\nDeep Wounds\nIn case of deep wound, it should be cleaned first with\ncotton dipped in an antiseptic solution.\nv Bleeding must be stopped by tying a bandage tightly\nabove the wound.\nAnother way to reduce the bleeding is always to apply a thick pad of cotton\nover the wound and press it with the thumb and fingers.\nNose Bleeding\nFollowing steps should be taken to give first aid in\ncase of nose bleeding :\nv Make the patient sit on a chair comfortably. Tilt his head\nback and ask him to keep his arms folded above his head.\nv Prepare an ice pack and keep on the patient’s\nnose. It will reduce bleeding.\nv Ask the patient to breathe through his mouth.\nv Take the patient to hospital if there is heavy bleeding.\nFires\nFire can be caused because of unsafe use of match sticks, candles, kerosene\nand fire crackers. Gas leakage in kitchen, electrical faults etc.\n53EVS-4\nv Tetanus is a disease caused when the skin is cut by a dirty or rusted object.\nv Anti-tetanus injection given within 24 hours of the accident prevents the\ninfection from spreading.\n\nFollowing are some safety rules to avoid fire break out :\nv Electrical wiring should also be checked regularly.\nv Keep petrol and kerosene oil in air-tight container\nand away from fire.\nv Avoid wearing synthetic clothes while working in\nthe kitchen.\nv Never forget to switch off the gas cylinder after\nuse.\nv Handle the match sticks, candles and crackers carefully.\nPutting out fires\nFire can be put out in the following ways :\nv Fire are devices to put out fires. extinguishers\nThey contain carbon dioxide (CO ) gas or 2\nfoam. The gas surrounds the fire and puts it off\nby cutting off the air supply.\nv Throw sand or mud over the fire caused by\npetrol.\nv For electric fire, switch off the mains immediately. Throw sand on the fire.\nDon’t throw water over a fire caused by electricity.\nv In case of big fire, call fire brigade by dialing ‘101’.\nPoisoning\nIn case of poisoning, do the following :\nv Try to make the vomit.victim\nv Rush the victim to a doctor.\n54EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. We should keep petrol in _______________.\n2. Fire can be caused due to _______________ and _______________.\n3. We can call fire brigade by dialing _______________.\n\nAnimal Bites\nThe saliva of some animals contains viruses. For example, dogs, cats, monkeys,\nwolves and jackals. The viruses enter the victim’s body\nthrough the animal’s saliva when these animals bite\nsomeone and they cause rabies to the victim.\nv Wash the wound with soap and water to wash away\ngerms.\nv Apply an antiseptic on the wound. Bandage the wound\nwith a sterile gauze to prevent the infection.\nv Take the victim to the doctor immediately. The doctor\nmay give anti-rabies injections.\nIn case of snake bite :\nv To stop the flow of poison along with blood to the heart\nand brain, apply a tight bandage just above the bite.\nv Keep the victim still movement will cause the poison to\nspread faster in the victim’s body.\nv The victim should be immediately taken to the doctor.\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option.3\n1. Burns are caused due to :\na) heat b) chemicals c) both a) & b)\n2. Which is the good conductor of electricity ?\na) Mud b) Sand c) Water\n55EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Accidents happen due to carelessness.\nD we should try to avoid accidents by following safety rules.\nD First-aid is the immediate help given to injured person.\nD Different kinds of injuries need different kinds of first-aid.\n\n3. Which is the common problem among children during summer ?\na) Sprain b) Wound c) Nose bleeding\n.4 How can you put off fire caused due to electrical fault ?\na) By Water b) By fire extinguisher c) By sand or mud\n5. A cloth sling can be used in case of :\na) burns b) fractures c) poisoning\nB. Write for true and for false statements.T F\n1. In case of a fracture, it must be ensured that the fractured part is not\nmoved.\n2. Sprain is caused due to crack in bone.\n3. We should wash our hands before giving first-aid to an injured person.\n4. The saliva of animals such as dogs and cats cause a dangerous\ndisease called rabies.\n5. Naphthalene balls are also poisonous.\n6. We should move a person with snake bite.\nC. Fill in the blanks.\n1. The saliva of animals contains . _____________\n2 , _____________. In case of small burns apply on the burnt area.\n3. _____________Any break or crack in the is called fracture.\n_____________.4. The immediate help given to an injured person is called\n5 _____________. If an animal bites, the doctor may give injections.\n6. Fire due to petrol can be extinguished by ______________.\nD. Answer the following questions.\n1. What is first-aid ?\n2. How would you help someone who has nose bleeding ?\n3. Name any three causes of fire.\n4. How would you help if a person get a deep wounds ?\n5. How would you help the person with snake bite ?\n6. What is fracture ? What first-aid should you give to an injured person ?\n56EVS-4\n\nA. Name the type of injury below each picture for which the first aid is being\nfollowed.\n_______________________ _______________________\nB. List some of the common accidents that can happen in the following places.\nPlace Accident\nIn school\nAt home\nOn road\nC. Visit a fire station with your friends and teacher. See there how a fire brigade\nworks and how they get to fire sites ? What equipments do they have ? Make a\nreport on the fire station in your scrapbook.\nD. Use your imagination and make a poster which shows that a little carefulness can\navoid an accident.\nE. A number of accidents on the road happens due to carelessness and not\nfollowing the rules. Accidents can also occur at home, in school and on any\npublic place due to carelessness of ourselves and some other person. We should\ntry to avoid them by following the safety rules.\n57EVS-4\nTASKS\n(Conceptual Learning)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Art Integration)\n(Life Skills)\n\nHere are some activities shown in the pictures. Write for the activities that yes\nshould be done and for the activities that should not be done.no\nv Salon is a four year old girl. She is very naughty. She wants to help her mother in i\nthe kitchen. But her mother does not allow her to come in the kitchen. Why ?\nv Why should we not operate any electrical switches when there is a gas leak ?\n58EVS-4\n\nFood is our basic need. We cannot live without food.\nWe need food for the following reasons :\nD Food gives us energy to work.\nD It protects us from diseases.\nD It keeps us healthy and active.\nFood from Plants\nMost of the food that we eat comes from the plants. Cereals, pulses, fruits,\nvegetables, spices, oil, etc., are all obtained from plants. We also get beverages\nlike tea and coffee from the plants.\nCereals and Pulses\nFood grains like rice, wheat, oats, maize and millets are called . Urad, cereals\ngram, arhar, kidney beans, etc., are pulses.\nCereals and pulses together are called food grains. Rice is a staple food in many\nparts of the country. Wheat flour is used to make chapatis, bread, etc.\nWheat Rice Maize Pulses Gram\nFruits and Vegetables\nFruits and vegetables are also obtained from the plants. They are full of vitamins\n9\nFood We Eat\n• Food from Plants • The Journey of Food\n• Food of Different Regions\n59EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. How the process of growing\ncrops begin ?\n2. Who prepares tasty food for you\nat home ?',5),(15,1,'Food','Unit 6 Food\n\nand minerals that are good for health. They protect us from diseases and keep\nus healthy. Mango, banana, cherry, orange, pineapple, papaya and grapes are\nsome popular fruits.\nFruits are eaten raw. We also drink the juices of different fruits. Peas, carrot,\nbottle gourd, potato, turnip and radish are some common vegetables. They are\neaten raw or after cooking.\nFruits Vegetables\nSpices\nCurry\nLeaves\nChillies\nCloves\nCinnamon\nCardamom\nBlack Pepper Turmeric\nSpices\nSpices are added to the food to give\nit flavour, taste and colour. We get\nspices from flowers, stem, fruits,\nbark, seeds and roots of the plants.\nOilseeds\nDifferent kinds of oils are used in different parts of\nIndia for cooking. Oil is extracted from special\nseeds called . Oilseeds are crushed to get oilseeds\noil. Groundnut, sesame, mustard, olive, sunflower\nand soyabean are some of the most commonly\nused oilseeds.\n60EVS-4\nRaisins, almonds, cashewnuts, pistachio and walnuts are some of the dry fruits we\neat.\n\nPloughing the Fields Sowing the Seeds Irrigating the Fields\nBeverages\nLeaves of some plants are used to make drinks. These drinks are called\nbeverages. Tea and coffee are popular beverages obtained from plants.\nThe Journey of Food\nThe journey of food starts with farmers,\nfishermen and dairy workers. Farmers grow\ncrop plants on large areas of land called\nfarms. The farmer first prepares the field\nby it. Small farmers use oxen to ploughing\nplough their fields, whereas big farmers\nuse tractors for the same.\nThen, he adds and to the soil to increase the yield of his manures fertilizers\ncrop. The second step is the seeds. After a few days, the seeds sowing\ngerminate and small plants appear.\nThe third step is the crops. The young plants are watered regularly irrigating\nand sprayed with pesticides and insecticides to keep them safe from insects.\nFarmer Grows Crops\n61EVS-4\nSpices like ginger, cloves and turmeric can be used as medicines.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. What we get from plants ?\n2. Which fruit do you like the most ?\n\nThe crops grow in about six months. They are then ready to be cut. The fourth\nstep is , i.e., cutting the crops.harvesting\nAfter the crops have been harvested, grains are separated from the plants. This\nis called . Removing the husk further cleans grains. This is called threshing\nwinnowing. Now, the crops are ready to be packed and sent to the market on\ntractor, truck or bullock-cart.\nHarvesting the Crop Threshing the Crop Winnowing the Crop\nThe crop is transported to the wholesale market or mandi. From the wholesale\nmarket, this food stuff is bought by the retailers. We buy the food stuff from the\nretailers.\nWholesalers buy and transport\nfood items from the fields to the\nwholesale markets.\nFarmers grow crops\nin their fields.\nRetailers and vendors buy\nfood items from mandis\nand sell them in local\nmarkets.\nThe food items are washed,\ncleaned and cooked at\nhome.\nWe eat and enjoy this\nfood together.\n62EVS-4\n\nFood of Different Regions\nDal-Bati\nChoorma from\nRajasthan\nMakki-Ki-Roti,\nSarson-Ka-Saag\nfrom Punjab\nFish Curry, Rice\nand Rasogulla\nfrom Bengal\nDhokla, Thepla\nand Khandvi from\nGujarat\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Which of these is not a grain ?\na) Wheat b) Rice\nc) Maize d) Clove\n2. Farmers start their farming activity with .____________\na) adding fertilizers b) irrigation\nc) ploughing d) sowing\n63EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Food is one of our most basic needs.\nD Plants are the main source of food.\nD Cereals and pulses together are called food grains.\nD Oil is extracted from special seeds called oilseeds.\nD Fruits and vegetables are also obtained from plants.\nD Spices are added to the food to give its flavour, taste and colour.\nD The journey of food has these steps :\n1. Ploughing the field 2. Sowing the seeds 3. Irrigating the field\n4. Harvesting the crop 5. Threshing the crop 6. Winnowing the crop\nD Different regions have their own special food items.\n\n3. Cardamom is a :\na) fruit b) oilseed\nc) spice d) pulses\n4. Wholesalers sell food to the :\na) retailers b) shopkeepers\nc) both a) & b) d) none of these\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. Most of the food that we eat comes from ._____________\n2. , , and are dry fruits.____________ ____________ ____________ ____________\n3. Manure is added to the soil to increase the ._____________\n4. Separating grains from the husk is called . _____________\nC. Give two examples of each :\n1. Fruits : _______________________ _______________________\n2. Vegetables : _______________________ _______________________\n3. Spices : _______________________ _______________________\n4. Beverages : _______________________ _______________________\nD. Write the journey of food in your own words in the space provided below :\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\nE. What are beverages ? Give some examples also :\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\nF. Answer the following questions :\n1. Can we live without food ? Why / Why not ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n64EVS-4\n\n2. What are food grains ? Explain it.\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What are beverages ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. What are oilseeds and how do we get oil from them ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n65EVS-4\nTASKS\n(Art Integration)\nMilk Rice\nFish\nYogurt\nBroccoliBeens\nBread\nBanana\nA. Make a Food Group Wheel on a card paper as shown below :\nCut a hole in the centre of the wheel. Take a spinner. Cut a small arrow shape on a\npaper. Paste it on one at the legs of the spinner. Now adjust the spinner in the\ncentre hole on the wheel. Spin the spinner and wait for it to stop determine the\nfood group of the food item in the direction of the arrow.\n\nB. Collect the samples of different spices in small plastic packets. Attach them in\nyour EVS notebook and label them.\nC. Collect an empty wrapper of any breakfast cereal. It can be oats, cornflakes,\nporridge or any other. Read the information on the wrapper and fill in the blanks :\n1. Name of the product : ________________________________________\n2. Name of the brand : ________________________________________\n3. Quantity : ________________________________________\n4. Rate : ________________________________________\n5. Content : ________________________________________\n6. Date of packing : ________________________________________\n7. Date of expiry : ________________________________________\nD. Write the names of the states of India to their correct famous food :\n1. 2. 3. 4.\n(Art Integration)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Knowledge of India)\nWe eat food to remain healthy. How should we ensure that we eat healthy food ?\nAt times, when we are not hungry, we waste food. But, there are so many people who\ndonʼt get any food to eat. How can you, as a student, help to solve this problem ?\n(Scientific Temper)\n(Problem Solving)\n66EVS-4\n\nWater is the basic need of all living organism. It includes human beings animals\nand plants. We all need clean water to drink and for other needs.\nAbout 97% of the Earth’s water is stored in sea and oceans. This water is salty\nand cannot be used for drinking only 3% is fresh water which we can drink.\nWater Pollution\nHarmful chemical wastes from homes and factories, fertilizers, pesticides,\nsewage and dead bodies make the water impure and unfit for drinking. This is\ncalled and the substances which cause pollution are called water pollution\npollutants.\nCauses of Water Pollution\nD Throwing dead bodies in water.\nD Oil spilled by ships and tankers.\nD Discharge of industrial wastes in water.\nD Washing clothes on the river banks.\nD Discharge of domestic wastes in water bodies.\nEffects of Water Pollution\nWhen polluted water is used by animals or human beings directly or indirectly —\nD It can cause diseases like typhoid, cholera, jaundice, diarrhoea, etc.\nD Aquatic animals become sick and die in polluted water.\nD Polluted water makes soil infertile and crops get damaged.\n10\nWater ‘O’ Water\n• Water Pollution • Causes of Water Pollution\n• Effects of Water Pollution • Every Drop Counts\n• Purification of Water\n67EVS-4',6),(16,1,'Water','Unit 7 Water\n\nD Water plants get infected and die in\npolluted water.\nD When infected aquatic animals are eaten\nby human beings or other animals, it\nmakes them sick and unhealthy.\nWe must check water pollution if we want to\nimprove the quality of water bodies. Water\npollution should be controlled because there is limited amount of fresh water\npresent on our Earth.\nEvery Drop Counts\nWater is precious. Although 70% of our Earth is covered with water, still\nall this water cannot be used for daily activities as\nit is saline. Fresh water is available in the form of\nunderground water, water in rivers, ponds and\nlakes. This is not sufficient for everyone. This is the\ncause of water shortage.\nThus, we need to be a little careful to conserve\nwater. Rainwater harvesting is a very good method\nto conserve rainwater and store it for later use. This is done in open in rural areas.\nRainwater Harvesting\nPurification of Water\nWater that is pure for drinking is called . Water from rivers, lakes, potable water\nreservoirs and other water bodies is brought through channels to the water-\ntreatment plant. Here, this water is made pure for drinking. At the water-\ntreatment plant, water is first filtered to remove the impurities. It is then treated\nwith chemicals to kill the germs present in it. This purified water is then\npumped into huge water tanks. From here, the water reaches to the taps in our\n68EVS-4\nFrozen water is slightly lighter than liquid water. That’s why we see ice floating on\nwater.\n\nhouses through a network of\npipelines. The height of the\ntank gives steady pressure\nto water passing through the\npipes to our taps.\nWater can be made safe at\nhome by the following\nmethods :\nBoiling\nBoiling kills the germs in water. Water should be\nboiled and cooled before drinking.\nUsing Chemicals\nChlorine tablets are added to water to kill germs in\nit. This process is called .chlorination\nFiltration\nSolid impurities like dirt and sand can be removed by filtering water. Now-a-\ndays, ultraviolet purifiers are used to make water pure for drinking.\nPeople use purifiers or Reverse Osmosis system to kill germs in water.\nWater Treatment Plant\n69EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Harmful chemical wastes from homes and factories, fertilizers,\npesticides, sewage and dead bodies pollute the water.\nD Polluted water disturbs the aquatic life by killing fish and water plants.\nD Polluted water can cause diseases like jaundice, typhoid, dysentery,\ndiarrhoea and cholera.\nD Water which is pure for drinking is called potable water.\nD We should be a little careful to conserve water.\nD We must make sure that the water we drink is clean.\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Water is the basic need of all organism.__________\na) living b) non-living\nc) thirsty d) none of these\n2. Which of these is a cause of water pollution ?\na) Bathing animals b) lmmersion of idols\nc) Dumping of wastes d) All of these\n3. About of Earth’s surface is covered with water.__________\na) 50% b) 60% c) 70% d) 80%\n4. How can we clean water ?\na) By boiling b) By adding chlorine tablets\nc) Both a) & b) d) None of these\nB. Write or :True False\n1. Water fit for drinking is called potable water.\n2. There is no need to cover the drinking water.\n3. Boiling cannot kill the germs present in water.\n4. Purifiers kill the germs present in water.\nC. Fill in the blanks :\n1. Throwing dead bodies in the causes water pollution.________________\n2. Water animals become and die.________________\n3. Pure water that we can drink is called water.________________\nD. Explain each in two sentences :\n1. Chlorination : ______________________________________________________\n___________________________________________________________________\n2. Filtration : __________________________________________________________\n___________________________________________________________________\n3. Boiling : ___________________________________________________________\n___________________________________________________________________\n70EVS-4\n\nE. Answer the following questions :\n1. What are different causes of water pollution ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What are the effects of water pollution ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What is rainwater harvesting ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. How much water is available on Earth ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Visit a water-treatment plant. Observe the different steps of water purification.\nB. Make a list of different types of water filters available in the market. Note their\nspecial features. Find out which is the most popular filter and why ?\nC. Write a note on ʻConservation of Waterʼ :\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nD. Discuss in the class about the factors causing pollution of these water bodies :\n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n(Communication Skills)\n(Environment & Health)\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Experiential Learning)\n71EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nAll of us need house. We know about its importance in our life. But building a\nhouse is not a simple work. It requires a lot of money, material and people.\nLand is the basic requirement for making a house.\nThere is a long list of materials that are required in making of a house. We need\nsand, cement, bricks, steel, stone, wood, glass, tiles, pipes, etc.\nMaking of Bricks\nRead these steps to know how bricks are made :\nStep 1 : First, the fine clay is dug.\nStep 2 : The clay is, then, mixed with water and\nkneaded till it becomes soft.\nStep 3 : The clay dough is, then, put in moulds and\ndried in the Sun.\nStep 4 : The dried bricks are, then, burnt in the kiln.\nStep 5 : Once, the bricks cool down, they are placed on\nthe pallets for storage.\nConstruction of a House\nMany people who are skilled in different jobs, work together to construct a\nhouse or a building.\nThe Architect\nThe is the first one who makes the design of the house or building on architect\na paper. It is called a . This plan shows the layout of the building.plan\n11 Houses and\nBridges\n• Making of Bricks • Construction of a House\n• Bridges\n72EVS-4',7),(17,1,'Shelter','Unit 8 Shelter\n\nThe Carpenter\nThe completes the wood work of the house. carpenter\nHe makes doors, windows, cupboards and furniture\nof the house. He uses tools like saw, gimlet, chisel,\nsquares for his work. He takes the help of his helpers\nto hold the wooden pieces while working.\nThe Plumber\nThe lays the water pipes and taps in the house. plumber\nHe lays the pots, sinks, wash basins, taps and pipes in the\nbathrooms and kitchen. He uses pliers, screw drivers,\nwrench, spanner, chisel, rubber fork cup, etc., for working.\nThe Electrician\nThe installs and maintains the electrical electrician\nconnections of the house. He fits electrical mains,\nelectrical points, plugs, switches safely in the\nbuilding. His main tools are screw drivers, pliers, drill,\nsoldering iron, insulation tape, etc.\nThe Mason\nThe lays the bricks to make walls, roofs and mason\npillars of the houses. He uses tools like trowel,\nhammer, mallet, plumb bot, etc., to make straight walls\nand pillars. Many labourers carry cement and bricks on\ntheir heads and bring it to the mason for work. Mason\nPainter\nThe Painter\nThe paints the building after all the other work are painter\ndone. He uses a ladder.\nThere are many other people who make things in their\nworkshops and deliver them at the construction site.\nThe makes the grills.welder\n73EVS-4\n\nSuspension Bridge\nBeam Bridge\nArch Bridge\nArch Bridge\nArch bridge looks like a semi-circle. It is a very\nstrong bridge made of semi-circular arches.\nEarlier, it was made of stones but now steel,\nconcrete and bricks are used.\nBridges\nThere are many types of bridges. Let’s discuss about them.\nSuspension Bridge\nSuspension bridge has enormous towers of steel\nand concrete set into their foundation. Chains or\nropes hang between the towers. are Footbridges\nsmall suspension bridges made from wood or\nrope which hang over small streams.\nBamboo Bridge\nBamboo bridges are commonly found in villages.\nCantilever Bridge\nA is a modified form cantilever bridge\nof the beam bridge. This type of bridge\nhas support only on one end.\nBeam Bridge\nA is the oldest type of bridge. It beam bridge\nconsist of a horizontal structure called a\nbeam which rests on support on either end.\nThe modern beam bridges are built of steel\nand concrete. A long beam bridge has many\npillars to support the beam.\n74EVS-4\nThe great pyramid of Giza was the tallest structures in the world for over 3800 years.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Why bridges are constructed ?\n2. Which is the most famous bridge\nof India ?\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. is the basic requirement for construction of a house.___________\na) Air b) Water c) Land d) Brick\n2. Construction of a house begin with .___________\na) a building plan b) approval from the government\nc) bricks d) concrete\n3. The electrical connections in the house are fixed by .___________\na) carpenter b) plumber\nc) electrician d) sweeper\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. A lays bricks to make the wall._________________\n2. The design of a building is created by an ._________________\n3. The dried bricks are burnt in a . _________________\n4. The helps the mason._________________\nC. Answer the following questions :\n1. How is a brick made ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. Name the different materials required in making a house.\n____________________________________________________________________\n75EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD For making a house lot of money, labour and material is required.\nD The basic material used to make houses, buildings and bridges is brick.\nD Bricks are made of clay.\nD Ancient houses were made of mud having thatched roofs.\nD Many people who are skilled in different jobs work together to\nconstruct a house or a building.\nD There are many types of bridges—suspension bridge, bamboo bridge,\ncantilever bridge, beam bridge and arch bridge.\n\n3. What is a cantilever bridge ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. Why do bridges need to be strong ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Make a chart on different types of bridges. Hang it in your classroom.\nB. Make a bridge with sticks over a shoe box.\nC. Effect of colour on room temperature :\nHow does the colour of your house affect the temperature inside ? To answer this\nquestion, let us do an experiment :\n1. Carefully cut all three construction paper into pieces so that it can cover the\nentire surface of one of the cardboard boxes.\n2. Use the glue to attach the paper to the box. It should be done as smoothly as\npossible so that there are no air pockets between the paper and the box\nbelow it.\n3. You should now have a white box, a grey box and a black box.\n4. Put the thermometer in the each box, close it and take it outside.\n5. Place the boxes in the sunny spot you picked. Leave them there for 30\nminutes.\n6. After 30 minutes, open the box and quickly note down the temperature on all\nthe thermometers.\nTip : You can try the same procedure in the shade also, still outside and note down\nthe difference in temperature.\nWhich box was the hottest after 30 minutes and which was the coolest ?\nResult : The white box reflects more light than the darker boxes. Therefore, it will\nshow the lowest temperature. The grey box will have higher temperature and the\nblack box will be warmest as it absorbs most of the energy.\nWhy is it necessary to have a proper ventilation system in a building ?\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Art Integration)\n(Creativity)\n(Critical Thinking)\n76EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nIn order to protect themselves from harsh weather and animals, early man lived\nin caves and on high trees. When they started a settled life, they started making\nhouses with muds, stones and straws. It used to be a single room house.\nGradually, separate space for storage of grains, for cooking and for bathing were\nadded. That was the beginning for the modern multi-room houses.\nKutcha House\nOne day, Neeta went to her grandfather’s village\nto attend the wedding of her cousin.\nHer grandfather said, “Neeta, did you notice that\nthe houses in the village are totally different\nfrom the houses in the cities ?”\n“Yes, Grandpa! But why is it so ?” asked Neeta.\nGrandfather replied, “This is because the houses\nin the village are mostly kutcha houses whereas\nin cities there are pucca houses.” Neeta had\nnever heard about kutcha and pucca houses\nearlier. So, she asked her grandfather to explain\nthe differences between these two houses.\nHouses in Rural Areas\nHouses in villages are very simple. They are\ncalled . These are temporary kutcha houses\nhouses made up of mud having thatched roof.\n12 House\nTransformation\n• Houses in Rural Areas • Houses in Urban Areas\n• Slums • Houses—Then and Now\n77EVS-4\n\nThe floor of such houses is smeared with a mixture of cow\ndung and mud. People can collect mud, stones,\nleaves, bamboo, etc., from their surroundings\nwithout spending any money.\nNow-a-days, some houses in rural areas are\nconstructed with better materials like bricks\nand cement. These houses can withstand\nstrong winds and rain. These are called\npucca houses.\nRich people in villages live in big pucca\nhouses known as . They are made havelis\nup of bricks, stones, clay, tiles and thick,\nheavy floors. The roofs are supported\nwith strong wooden beams. Unlike huts,\nthey have many big spacious rooms.\nThere is an or in the aangan courtyard\nmiddle of the haveli.\nHaveli\nPucca House\nHouses in Urban Areas\nAll the houses in urban areas are pucca\nhouses. These are made up of bricks,\niron and steel, cement, tiles, glass and\nmetals. They are very durable and\nstrong houses.\nMulti-Storey Buildings\nNow-a-days, cities are occupied by\nmany multi-storey buildings. These are\ntall buildings with a number of floors.\nEach floor has a number of flats.\nMulti-Storey Building\n78EVS-4\n\nSlums\nMany people who cannot afford to buy or\nbuild houses for themselves, live in these\nslums. A is a cluster of huts built close slum\nto one another in a city because of shortage\nof space. They make kutcha houses with\ncheap and readily available materials.\nSometimes, they even use waste materials\nto make their huts. Slums are totally\nunplanned, unsafe and unhygienic.\nHouses—Then and Now\nIn earlier times, bricks, mud and wood were\nthe common materials used to build a house. Lantern and earthen lamps were\nused for lighting. Very few people had electric connections in their houses. The\nhouses had high roofs and plenty of doors and windows for ventilations. There\nused to be an open space in the centre of the house, where family members\nand guests used to sit on the cots. Sewer\npipes were made of clay. They led from\nbathrooms to sewers and drained into the\nnearby rivers and streams. It was generally a\njoint family system and all the women of the\nhouse used to work together in one kitchen.\nThe breaking down trend of joint families to\nnuclear families brought changes in houses.\nNow builders are also making small houses\nto cater to the requirements of smaller\nnuclear families. The types of houses being\nbuilt today depend on the region and\nclimate of the area.\nSlums\nHaveli\nFlats\n79EVS-4\nDharavi in Mumbai is one of the\nlargest slums in Asia.\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. All houses found in cities are houses.____________\na) kutcha b) pucca c) temporary d) tent\n2. The type of the houses built also depend on the of the area.____________\na) weather b) looks\nc) climate d) None of these\n3. Where are havelis found ?\na) Cities b) Towns c) Villages d) All of these\n4. is breaking down the trend of joint families. ___________\na) Big family b) Community\nc) Nuclear family d) All of these\nC. Match the following :\n1. Pucca houses a) Flat\n2. Kutcha houses b) Ancient house\n3. Slum c) Bricks and concrete\n4. Multi-storey building d) Cluster of huts\n5. Haveli e) Mud\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. and were the first shelter for early man.____________ ____________\n2. As most of the families are nuclear now, houses are also becoming .________\n3. and were used for lighting. ____________ ____________\n80EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Houses in village are simple.\nD Kutcha houses are made up of mud and often need to be repaired.\nD Pucca houses are strong houses made up of bricks, cement, iron and\nsteel, glass, wood, metals, etc.\nD A slum is a cluster of huts built close to one another in a city because\nof shortage of space.\n\n4. Cities are occupied by many buildings.____________\n5. Sewer pipes were made of .____________\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. How men started making houses ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What is a pucca house ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What are different types of houses in cities ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. Describe a slum.\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. With the help of your teacher, organize a visit to a slum area. Talk to the people\nliving there and find out the answers of the following questions :\n1. What materials have you used to make your houses ?\n2. Where do you get drinking water from ?\n3. Do the children living here go to school ?\n4. How do you get electricity ?\n5. What kind of house do you wish for yourself ?\nB. Visit a construction site. Observe the different materials used there. Select\nsamples of these materials and put them in transparent bags. Bring them to the\nclass and show these materials to your classmates.\nC. Write a poem on your house here.\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nMany people in India are poor and homeless. Does giving them some money help to\nsolve their problem ? (Critical Thinking)\n(Multidisciplinary Learning)\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Creativity)\n81EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nRaj wants to visit Habeeb’s house to play cricket. Habeeb kept on explaining him\nthe location of his house but Raj could not understand. Then, Habeeb made a\nmap of his neighbourhood with , showing various , which symbols landmarks\neasily guided Raj to his place. This is the map of Habeeb’s neighbourhood.\nSchool\nHospital\nPost Office\nHouses\nBus Stop\nPark\nSymbols\n+\nBUS\nPark\n+\n13 Mapping\nNeighbourhood\n• Maps • Scale\n• Directions • Compass\n82EVS-4',8),(18,1,'Travel and Communication','Unit 9 Travel and Communication\n\nHabeeb explained to Raj, “From the school go straight to the hospital and turn\nright. You will see a post office there, on the opposite side of the bus stand.\nKeep walking till you see a group of houses on your right. The road ends there.\nTake a right turn from here till you reach a park. Here, you will see another\ngroup of houses on your left. Mine is the second house from the corner.”\nMaps\nA is a drawing of any place—a map\nbuilding, a colony, a village, a town, a\ncountry or world. Map are useful to find\nthe location of a place. A map is much\nsmaller than the size of the actual area of the place which it depicts. A map is\nmade according to a . It is the ratio of the distance between two places as scale\ndrawn on the map and the actual distance between them on the ground. A\nbook of map is called an .atlas\nA map which shows the different states of countries is called a . A political map\nmap which tells us about the physical features of a place is called a .physical map\nDifferent signs and symbols are used on the maps to show various features like\nroads, towns, cities, bridges, streams, forests, dams, wells, post office, etc.\nSymbols are drawn in different colours to show the different landforms and water\nbodies.\nSome symbols are given in the table below :\nInternational\nBoundary\nState\nBoundary\nDistrict\nBoundary\nMetalled Road\nSea Route\nMasjid\nChurch\nTemple\nDam Grass and\nShrub\nA person who studies or makes maps\nis called a cartographer and map\nmaking is called cartography.\n83EVS-4\n\nSigns and symbols used on a map\nRiver or\nStream\nTown or\nVillage\nWell and\nTank\nCapital of\nthe Country\nRailway\nBroad Gauge\nRailway\nNarrow Gauge\nScale\nMaps represent a large area of land in a small drawing, so they use a scale. Map\nscale is the relationship between distance on a map and actual distances on the\nground.\nFor instance, if the distance between two cities is 200 km, we can show it on a\nmap as 2 cm.\nIf, 200 km = 2 cm.\nThen, our scale is 100 km = 1 cm.\nScales can vary from map to map. A map always contains a scale that is uses.\nDirections\nFour major directions are marked on a map. These are— , , andNorth South East\nWest. The top of the map shows the North and the bottom of the map shows\nthe South. East is to the right side of the map and West is to the left side of the\nmap.\nThe four sub-directions are andnorth-east, north-west, south-east\nsouth-west.\nBridge Forest\n84EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. How Habeeb made it simple to understand for Raj to reach his house ?\n2. Why can’t we use the actual size map ?\n\nCompass\nCompass is an instrument that helps\nus to know the directions. It has a\npointer which always points in the\nNorth direction.\nSouth (S)\nWest (W) East (E)\nNorth (N)\nNorth-West\n(NW)\nNorth-East\n(NE)\nSouth-West\n(SW)\nSouth-East\n(SE)\nMajor and Sub-directions\nTick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. An atlas is a book of ._____________\na) maps b) maths\nc) symbols d) directions\n2. Which of these can be shown as a symbol in a map ?\na) River b) Road\nc) Building d) All of these\n3. Which direction lies between East and South ?\na) north-east b) south-west\nc) south-east d) north-west\n85EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD A map has colours, directions, scale and symbols to represent the\ndetails of a place.\nD Maps represent a large area of land in small drawing, so they use a\nscale.\nD The four major directions are North, South, East, and West.\nD The four sub-directions are north-east, north-west, south-east and\nsouth-west.\nD Compass is an instrument that helps us to know the direction.\n\n4. are used on the maps to show road, bridge, town, etc._____________\na) Atlas b) Symbols\nc) Direction d) Scale\n86EVS-4\nTASKS\nA. Look at this map and answer the questions that follow :\n1. Which place is between the School and the Hospital ?\n_____________________________________________________________________\n2. What is towards the right of the Houses ? _______________________________\n3. Where do we reach if we cross the Raman Road from the Park ?\n_____________________________________________________________________\n4. Describe the location of the Park.\n_____________________________________________________________________\n_____________________________________________________________________\n5. Reach Market from School using two different ways in the map above.\nNehru Road\nRaman Road\nVijay Road\n(Critical Thinking)\nB. Write the meaning of the following colours used on a maps :\n1. 2.\n3. 4.\nPurple\nGreen\nBrown\nRed\n(Conceptual Learning)\n\nC. Take a political map of India with neighbouring countries. Now write the\ndirection of each neighbouring country on the map.\nD. Draw the map of your neighbourhood in your scrapbook. Mark and name the\nimportant landmarks on the map.\nE. A compass is a tool for finding direction. A simple compass is a magnetic needle\nmounted on a pivot, or short pin. We can make it at home also :\nD Cut out a large cardboard circle that is about an inch wider on each side than\nthe mouth of the cup.\nD Cut a smaller circle from the center of the large circle. The smaller circle\nshould be just a bit smaller than the mouth of the cup.\nD Divide the remaining doughnut into four equal quadrants, and label the\nquadrants with the letters N, S, E and W.\nD Stroke a needle with a magnet from eye to tip 30 times. Make sure to stroke it\nin the same direction each time.\nD Cut a cork lengthwise down the middle. Lay the cork so that its new flat (cut)\nsurface faces upwards.\nD Tape the needle to the flat surface, facing it in the same direction as the cork.\nD Fill the cup with water, almost to the rim.\nD Place the doughnut cutout over the mouth\nof the cup, so that the hole is directly over\nthe water.\nD Place the cork on the waterʼs surface so\nthat the needle is pointing from North to\nSouth.\nD Try out your compass, and compare it\nwith the results of a real compass.\n(Art Integration)\n(Multidisciplinary Learning)\n(Knowledge of India)\n87EVS-4\n\nEarly men used to travel on foot and animals. Later they invented wheels that\nhelped them travel to longer distances in shorter time. The people who lived\nnear rivers and sea coasts, developed rafts and boats.\nGoing forward, with the development in the field of science and technology\nmany advanced means of travel were developed. And today, we can cover\nthousands of kilometres in few hours.\nPrivate Means of Transport\nVehicles like cars, scooters, mopeds, motorcycle, etc., which are owned by us\nare called . We do not have to buy tickets for travelling private means of transport\nby a vehicle owned by us. We only have to fill fuel in it and we can go anywhere.\nMotorcycle Car Scooter\nPublic Means of Transport\nVehicles like bus, train, taxi, auto-rickshaw, ships and aeroplanes which are not\nowned by us are known as . We cannot use them as public means of transport\nand when we want. We have to buy tickets or pay some money for travelling by\nthem. Public means of transport are most useful for travelling to far off places.\nFor travelling by public transport, we have to buy tickets. We have to spend\nmoney to buy tickets.\n14 Currency and\nTransport\n• Means of Transport Private and Public • Currency\n• Travelling by Train, Metro and Aeroplane\n88EVS-4\n\nCurrency\nEvery country has a particular type of money that is used to buy things in that\ncountry. It is called the of that country. The currency of India is currency Indian\nRupees (`). The picture of ‘Gandhiji’ is printed on all the currency notes of India.\nIt is printed as a sign of respect to the ‘Father of the Nation’. Currencies made by\nmetals are called and those made of paper coins\nare called . notes\nEach coin has two sides—one side has its value\nmarked on it and the other side has the National\nEmblem. Some coins even have its value and the\nNational Emblem marked on the same side.\nCoins of Different\nDenominations\nTravelling by Train\nFor travelling by train, we have to buy tickets from the ticket counter at the\nrailway station. If we have to travel a long distance, then we should reserve a\nberth in advance. Long distance trains have many coaches like first class AC, 3rd\nclass AC, sleeper, general, etc.\nTicket counter Ticket\n89EVS-4\nCollection and study of coins of different countries is called numismatics.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Why do we pay for travel ?\n2. How can we identify Indian currency ?\n\nTravelling by Aeroplane\nWe need a passport if we want to visit a foreign country.\nPassport is a document given by the Government of India. The foreign visit is\nalso approved by the Government of India. This is known as .Visa\nWe can travel within India by\nair without a passport. Air\ntravel is an expensive means\nof travel.\nPeople may have to travel\nfrom one place to another\nfor various reasons. They\nmay buy goods from one place and sell it in another\nplace or they may go in search of work or even they\nmight move to a place that has more work opportunities.\nEarlier, travelling was much slower because proper roads were not built. Today,\nwe can travel very fast because of proper road, rail and air transport.\nE-Tickets\nIndian Railway’s Reservation website allows the passengers to buy tickets\nonline and pay by credit or debit cards. Passengers can also check train fares,\ntrain routes, availability, reservation and confirmation of tickets on this site.\nE-tickiting saves us from standing in a long queue for tickets.\nTravelling by Metro Train\nThousands of people travel by Metro Train\neveryday. We can buy for travelling to tokens\nany distance. Regular passengers use smart\ncards. They put the card at the sensing\ndevice on the entry and exit gates and the\nfare is deducted from the card’s account. Metro Train\nAeroplane\nPassport\n90EVS-4\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Early man travelled on foot and on ._______________\na) vehicles b) animals\nc) birds d) none of these\n2. Long distance trains have in them._______________\na) boxes b) coaches c) boggies d) chambers\n3. We need tickets for travelling by means of transport. ______________\na) public b) private c) school d) family\n4. It is a private means of transport.\na) Car b) Scooter c) Cycle d) All of these\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. Bus, train, taxi, ships, aeroplanes, all are examples of . _________________\n2. Coins are made of ._________________\n3. Every country has its own . _________________\n4. We can travel by Metro using or ._________________ _________________\n5. Air travel is an means of travel._________________\nC. Answer the following questions :\n1. What brought about a big change in travelling and how ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n91EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Travel and transports was revolutionzed by the invention of wheel.\nD Today, we have many different and advanced means of travel.\nD For travelling by public transport, we have to buy tickets.\nD The Indian currency consists of coins and notes.\nD E-ticketing saves us from standing in a long queue for tickets.\nD Air travel is an expensive means of travel.\nD Passport is a document given by the Government of India.\nD Thousands of people travel by metro train everyday.\n\n2. What is the difference between public and private transport ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What is the difference between a coin and a note ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. What is the national emblem of India ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Discuss about the usage of fake notes and its effect.\nB. Find the names of the currencies of the following countries and write their\nnames :\nCountry Currency\n1. Bangladesh ____________________________\n2. Bhutan ____________________________\n3. Sri Lanka ____________________________\n4. Pakistan ____________________________\n5. USA ____________________________\n6. UK ____________________________\nC. Draw a picture of any public transport here :\nWhat do you mean by plastic money ? How it is good or bad for us ? (Critical Thinking)\n(Art Integration)\n(Communication Skills)\n(Critical Thinking)\n92EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3 5\n1. A group consisting of parents and their children living together as a unit is called a :\na) Family b) Team\nc) Class d) Staff\n2. A potter makes pots for us._________\na) iron b) copper\nc) wooden d) silver\n3. Birds have in place of their hands._________\na) feathers b) wings\nc) beak d) tongue\n4. A group of elephants is called :\na) Society b) Herd\nc) Locality d) None of these\n5. is the kitchen of a plant._________\na) Root b) Stem\nc) Leaf d) Fruit\nB. Fill in the blanks : 5\n1. A group of people related by marriage, birth and adoption is called a .___________\n2. Birds body is made up of three parts head, trunk and ._____________\n3. An has big external ears._____________\n4. We play games to our mind._____________\n5. The saliva of animals contains ._____________\nC. Write or : True False 5\n1. A nuclear family is also known as extended family.\n2. We need special education to become a professional.\n3. Crow and pigeon fly at great heights.\n4. The colourful petals attract insects like butterflies, etc.\n5. Flowers grow from the fruits.\nTime : 1 hr. M.M. : 40(Based on Chapters–1 to 7) 1\n93EVS-4\n\nD. Match the following : 5\n1. Ostrich a) Scratching bird\n2. Sparrow b) Bird of prey\n3. Hen c) Perching bird\n4. Crane d) Flightless bird\n5. Hawk e) Wading bird\nE. Name the following : 5\n1. Part of the plant that grows above ground. ___________________\n2. Roots that grow out of branches of large tree. ___________________\n3. Most beautiful part of a plant. ___________________\n4. Process of making food by the plants. ___________________\n5. Kitchen of the plant. ___________________\nF. Look at the picture below and write the correct name of the parts of the flower : 5\nG. Answer the following questions : 10\n1. What is a joint family ?\n2. What service do we get from an electrician ?\n3. What is the most important and unique feature of birds ?\n4. Who becomes the leader of the group ?\n5. How plants are important for us and our environment ?\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n5.\n1. _____________\n2. _____________\n3. _____________\n4. _____________\n5. _____________\n94EVS-4\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option : 3 5\n1. Burns are caused due to :\na) heat b) chemicals\nc) sun d) none of these\n2. Farmers start their farming activity with .__________\na) sowing b) adding fertilizers\nc) irrigation d) cropping\n3. Water is the basic need of all organism.__________\na) living b) non-living\nc) human-like d) none of these\n4. The electrical connections in the house are fixed by .__________\na) electrician b) carpenter\nc) sweeper d) doctor\n5. is breaking down the trend of joint families.__________\na) Big family b) Community\nc) Nuclear family d) Society\nB. Fill in the blanks : 5\n1. The saliva of animals contains ._____________\n2. Most of the food that we eat comes from ._____________\n3. Pure water that we can drink is called water._____________\n4. A lays bricks to make the wall._____________\n5. and were used for lifting._____________ _____________\nC. Write or : True False 5\n1. We should wash our hands before giving first-aid to an injured person.\n2. Water fit for drinking is called potable water.\n3. We should move a person with snake bite.\n4. Purifiers kill the germs present in water.\n5. Boiling cannot kill the germs present in water.\nTime : 1 hr. M.M. : 40(Based on Chapters–8 to 14) 2\n95EVS-4\n\nD. Give two examples of each : 5\n1. Fruits : ________________ ________________\n2. Vegetables : ________________ ________________\n3. Spices : ________________ ________________\n4. Beverages : ________________ ________________\n5. Pulses : ________________ ________________\nE. Match the following : 5\n1. Pucca houses a) Flat\n2. Kutcha houses b) Ancient house\n3. Slum c) Bricks and concrete\n4. Multi-Storey building d) Cluster of huts\n5. Haveli e) Mud\nF. Look at the following symbols and write their names : 5\nG. Answer the following questions : 10\n1. How would you help someone who has nose bleeding ?\n2. What are food grains ? explain it.\n3. What are different causes of water pollution ?\n4. What is cantilever bridge ?\n5. How men started making houses ?\n1.\n__________ 2.\n3.4.\n5.\n__________\n__________\n__________\n__________\n96EVS-4',9);
INSERT INTO `lessons` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `title`, `content`, `sort_order`) VALUES (543,10,'Family and Local Institutions','Unit 1 Family and Local Institutions\n\nNow-a-days, there are very few joint families. Most families are splitting up due\nto the movement of the members of the family to other parts of the country for\njobs, for business or education. These members meet each other on special\noccasions like marriage, birthday parties and other family functions.\nThe members of a family are related to one another in the following ways :\nBy Marriage\n(Husband and wife\nstart a family\nafter marriage.)\nBy Birth\n(Children born to parents\nare related to their\nparents’ families.)\nBy Adoption\n(Sometimes, children are\nadopted into families even\nwhen they are not born to them.\nThen, they become a part\nof the family.)Importance of a Family\nA family lays the foundation of a human being. A family gives us an identity. Our\nparents teach us what is wrong and what is right.\nD Our family is our first school and our parents\nare our first teacher. They teach us good\nvalues.\nD Our family gives us strength to face the\nworld outside.\nD It fulfils all our daily needs.\nD Family members teach and serve one\nanother and also share joys and sorrows of life.\nD It provides love, support and a framework of values to each of its\nmembers.\n6EVS-4\n\nFamily Values\nSome values that we learn from our family are as follow :\nD To be truthful and honest.\nD To respect and obey elders.\nD To help the poor and needy people.\nD To share things with others.\nD To be sincere in all the work that we do.\nD To be polite with all.\nD To keep our house and surroundings clean.\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. A group consisting of parents and their children living together as a unit is\ncalled a :\na) family b) team c) class d) friends\nD A patriarchal family is the one in which a male is the head of the family.\nD A matriarchal family is the one in which a female is the head of the family.\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD A group of people closely related by blood, living together under one\nroof is called a family.\nD A family where a father, a mother and their children live together is\ncalled a nuclear family.\nD A family where more than two generations live together under one\nroof is called a joint family.\nD The members of a family are related to each other by marriage, birth\nor adoption.\nD A family lays the foundation of a human society.\n7EVS-4\n\n2. Joint family is also known as :\na) nuclear family b) extended family\nc) immediate family d) small family\n3. A family gives us :\na) family b) friends\nc) relatives d) an identity\n4. We should talk with everyone :\na) angrily b) politely\nc) sadly d) all of these\nB. Write or :True False\n1. A nuclear family is also known as extended family.\n2. A family is the basic unit of the society.\n3. Husband and wife start a family after marriage.\n4. We should be polite with all.\n5. Family can be big or small.\nC. Fill in the blanks :\n1. A group of people related by marriage, birth and adoption is called a\n____________.\n2. In a joint family, there are more than two live together.___________\n3. Families are spitting up due to the of the members of the family ___________\nto different parts of the .___________\n4. Our family is our first . ____________\n5. A family lays the foundation of a . ____________\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is a nuclear family ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What is a joint family ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What is adoption in families ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n8EVS-4\n\n4. How families are spitting up ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. Distinguish between a patriarchal family and matriarchal family.\n____________________________________________________________________\nYour photograph Your parentsʼ childhood\nphotograph\nDo you see any resemblance ?\nDiscuss and write :\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\nB. Draw your family tree on a chart paper. Paste and label photographs. Use different\ncolours to mark out your ʻimmediateʼ and ʻextendedʼ families.\nWe should love and care our family members. Every person in the family is equally\nimportant. We should always try to make them happy and feel special.\nSuhani forgot to wish her father on his birthday. Her father does not say anything but\nhe is sad. What should Suhani do ?\nDo you know any family where a child is adopted ?\n(Life Skills)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Art Integration)\nTASKS\nA. Paste your picture and your parentsʼ childhood pictures here : (Art Integration)\n9EVS-4\n\nWhat is Occupation ?\nWe need many products and services in our daily life. Like we need grocery, we\nneed milk, we need medicines, we need clothes, we need electronics items, we\nneed cleaning and repairing services and so on. We get these products and\nservices from different people. There are other people who work as teachers,\nlawyers, doctors, engineer or accountant, etc.\nBut why all those people do that work ? They do it to earn money. Jobs that\npeople do to earn money is called their .occupation\nJobs or occupations that need special training and education are called\nprofessions professionals. People who do these jobs are called . Doctors,\nengineers, pilots, teachers and architects are professionals. Some jobs need\nspecial training like that of a mechanic, potter, tailor and carpenter.\nLet us meet a few people at work to take a look at the jobs they do and the\ntools they use :\nTypes of Occupations\nThis is Raghu. He is a . He makes beautiful potter\nclay pots. He uses soft clay on a spinning wheel\nand gives different shapes to his pots. He also\nmakes small diyas which are used during\nDiwali. His wife helps him to paint the pots and\ndiyas.\nPotter\n2\nOccupations and\nProfessions\n• What is Occupation ? • Types of Occupations\n• Gender and Work\n10EVS-4\n\nThis is Nathu. He is a . He stitches clothes. He learnt tailor\nstitching from his mother. He uses a sewing machine to\nstitch the clothes.\nTailor\nBrij is a . He works in a motor mechanic\ngarage. He started his career as a helper.\nOver the years, he learnt the art of repairing\ncars and other vehicles. Now, he is the chief\nmechanic. People love and admire him for\nhis hard work and sincerity.\nMechanic\nPinku is a . He mends shoes, polishes cobbler\nthem and repairs the zips of bags and purses.\nPinku learnt this work from his father in his\nchildhood.\nCobbler\nPilot\nAmit is a . Since his childhood, he pilot\nused to play with aeroplanes. After\ncompleting his higher secondary\neducation, he joined a flying club to learn\nflying. After completing his training, he\ngot a license to fly. Now, he is a working\nas a pilot with Air India and takes\npassengers from one country to another.\n11EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. What is the occupation of your father or mother ?\n2. Do all occupations need specialised education ?\n\nDoctor\nManoj is a . He treats the sick people. He doctor\nuses a stethoscope, thermometer, etc., to\ncheck the patients. He diagnoses various\ndiseases and prescribes medicines. His\nparents were famous doctors and so is he.\nBesides these, there are many other people whose work is very important for\nus :\nD teacher A teaches in the school.\nD farmerA grows food grains.\nD architectAn makes designs of buildings.\nD driver A drives a vehicle.\nGender and Work\nIn the past, women were not encouraged to go to schools and colleges. They\nwere also not allowed to take up a job. However, time has changed now. Now,\nwomen are working in different fields as doctors, teachers, engineers,\nscientists, software programmers, architects, pilots, etc.\nWomen have reached the topmost position also in our country.\nPratibha Patil was the first woman President of our country in 2007 and\nDroupadi Murmu became the second woman President in 2022.\nSania Mirza, Sunita Williams, Kiran Bedi, etc., are some of very famous Indian\nwomen from different fields.\nDroupadi Murmu Sania Mirza Sunita Williams Kiran Bedi\n12EVS-4\nYogita Raghuwanshi is the first\nwoman truck driver of India.\n\nSimilarly, men have also ventured beyond their traditional fields of work. They\nnow work as chefs, fashion designers, hair stylists and make up artists.\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Who works in a hospital ?\na) Policeman b) Lawyer c) Doctor d) Tailor\n2. A potter makes pots for us.____________\na) iron b) copper\nc) earthen d) wooden\n3. A pilot flies a/an :\na) kite b) aeroplane\nc) both a) & b) d) none of these\n4. A barber :\na) mends our shoes b) stitches our clothes\nc) cuts our hair d) treats sick people\nB. Write or :True False\n1. We need special education to become a professional.\n2. We do not need any training and skill to become a potter.\n13EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Different people do different kinds of jobs to earn money.\nD Any work that we do to earn money is called occupation.\nD Jobs that need special training and education are called professions.\nD Some people work as farmers, carpenters, doctors, teachers,\nplumbers, cobblers, green grocers, tailors, nurses, etc.\nD Today, gender plays no role in the selection of an occupation.\nRAPIDTASK\nName any two famous Indian men who have ventured beyond their traditional\nfields of work.\n\n3. A cobbler mends and polishes our shoes.\n4. Only men can reach the top positions in our country.\nC. Whose help will you take :\n1. If your water pipeline is faulty. _______________________________\n2. If someone in intentionally hurts you. _______________________________\n3. If your car needs repairing. _______________________________\n4. If you want to get a dress stitched. _______________________________\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is an occupation ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. Why people work for others ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What service do we get from an electrician ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. What is the job of a lawyer ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. What does a carpenter do ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. There must be many people in your neighbourhood who work as cobbler, potter,\nmechanic, electrician, milkman, washerman, driver, etc. Take an interview of\nany one of them :\nYou can frame such questions.\n1. What is your name ? ___________________________\n2. What work do you do ? ___________________________\n3. From whom did you learn this skill from ? ___________________________\n4. Do you like your work ? ___________________________\n5. How many hours do you work everyday ? ___________________________\n6. Are you able to earn enough ? ___________________________\n(Vocational Skills)\n14EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nDo you think that all the sections of the society including SC, ST, minorities and\nwomen are getting equal opportunity to work ?\nB. Gather information about these professions :\n1. 2. 3.\nEmbroidery Wood Craft Carpet Making\nC. Collect the pictures of special tools that are used in professions given above and\nstick them here :\n• We all should do our work with honesty and sincerity.\n• Do you think every work is important. Whether it is highly paid or lowly paid ?\nWhy / Why not ?\n(Art Integration)\n(Democratic and Human Values)\n(Democratic and Human Values)\n(Experiential Learning)\n15EVS-4\n\nBirds are a beautiful animal. They can fly. Their body is divided into three parts\n– head, trunk, and tail. Birds have legs but no hand. They have wings in place of\ntheir hands.\nThere are more than 9000 species of birds.\nThey are spread over the entire Earth. They are\nof different sizes and many different colours.\nThere whole body is covered with feathers.\nFeathers, beak and claws are their special\nfeatures.\nHead part\nTrunk part\nTail part\nDown Feathers\nThese feathers cover the\nbody and keep it warm.\nThey are small and\nfluffy.\nBody Feathers\nThese feathers cover the\nbody and give a shape\nto it.\nFlight Feathers\nFlight feathers are long\nand flat feathers in the\nwings and tail. They help\nthe bird to fly.\nDifferent Kinds of Feathers\nFeathers\nBirds are the only animals which have feathers. A bird’s body is covered with\nfeathers. Feathers are of different colours in different birds. There are three\nkinds of feathers.\n3\nAmazing Birds\n• Feathers • How do Birds Fly ? • Flying Differently\n• Flightless Birds • Aquatic Birds • Feet and Claws of Birds\n• Birds of Prey • Beaks and Food Habits of Birds\n16EVS-4',1),(544,11,'Birds and Animals','Unit 2 Birds and Animals\n\nHow do Birds Fly ?\nBirds fly with the help of movement of wings. A bird flaps its wings in both\ndirections, upward and the downward, to fly. These movements of wings are\ncalled and respectively.upstroke downstroke\nUpstroke Downstroke\nMovements of Wings of a Flying Bird\nWhen a bird reaches higher in the air, it does not need to flap its wings. The tail\nof a bird acts like the rudder of a boat. It helps the bird to change its direction\nduring the flight.\nFlying Differently\nSome birds like eagle, vulture and\nkite fly at great heights.\nSome birds like sparrow and bulbul fly low.\nSome birds like crow and pigeon fly over\nlong distances.\nSparrow\nKite Eagle\nPigeonCrowHen\nSome birds like hen fly only short\ndistances. They usually walk on legs.\nVulture\n17EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. What is the most special feature of birds ?\n2. What helps the birds in flying ?\n\nFlightless Birds\nSome birds like kiwi, ostrich, penguin do not fly at all because their bodies are\ntoo heavy. These birds are called . They move on with the help of flightless birds\ntheir legs.\nKiwi Penguin Ostrich\nAquatic Birds\nAquatic birds live in water. They spend most of their time in water, either\nfloating on the surface, diving to catch fish, swimming gracefully or simply\nwading through the water. These birds have oil glands which do not allow their\nfeathers to get wet or spoiled by water. These birds have webbed toes which\nhelp them to swim in water. Ducks, swans, cranes, gulls and pelicans are\ncommon water birds.\nFeet and Claws of Birds\nDifferent birds have different types of feet and claws.\nThe claws of a bird help the bird in perching, walking, climbing and swimming.\nThey also help in catching, holding and eating the food. Claws protect the birds\nfrom their enemies.\nDuck Swan PelicanCrane\n18EVS-4\n\nLet us know about some common types of feet and claws of birds :\nScratching Birds\nScratching birds like hens and quails have strong feet\nwith three toes in front and one short toe at the back.\nEach of these toes has a sharp horny claw. These birds\nscratch the ground to bring out the buried food, seeds\nand insects from the ground. Scratching birds walk and\nrun on the ground.\nPerching Birds\nPerching birds like crow and sparrow have four toes in\ntheir feet— three in front and one at the back. These toes\nhelp such birds in perching, that is to hold the branch of\na tree very firmly even when the bird is asleep.\nPerching birds may also hop on the ground.\nBirds of Prey\nBirds of prey like eagle, vulture, hawk and owl have\nvery strong feet with sharp claws called to catch talons\nand hold their prey firmly. These birds may carry their\nprey to long distances.\nSwimming Birds\nSwimming birds like duck and swan have webbed feet.\nTheir feet have special webs of skin between toes. A\nwebbed foot is like a paddle. Webbed toes help birds to\npush back water while swimming.\nClimbing Birds\nClimbing birds like parrots and woodpeckers have four\ntoes—two pointing upwards and two downwards.\nThese toes help the bird to cling to the bark of trees\nand to climb up.\nA Scratching Bird\nand its Toes\nA Perching Bird\nand its Toes\nA Climbing Bird\nand its Toes\nA Bird of Prey\nand its Toes\nA Swimming Bird\nand its Toes\n19EVS-4\n\nA Wading Bird\nand its Toes\nPeacock\nShort and Broad Beak\nof a Swallow\nWading Birds\nWading birds like crane and paddy birds have thin and\nlong feet with wide spreading toes. These toes keep the\nfeet safe from sinking in the soft mud.\nBeaks and Food Habits of Birds\nBirds eat grains, seed, fruits and flesh. They use their\nbeak to catch and hold food. Birds do not have hands\nand teeth. Their beak tear, bite, chisel and crush the\nfood. Different birds have different types of beak. Its\nshape and size depend on their food habits.\nSome birds used their beaks to fight with the enemy or catch the prey or use it\nto clean the feathers. According to their food habits, beaks are of different types.\nShort and Broad Beak\nBirds like swallows have short and broad beaks which are\nsticky inside. These birds move round and round in the air\nkeeping their beaks open. During flight the small flying\ninsects enter the beak and get stuck inside the mouth.\nShort, Hard and Horny Beak\nBirds like sparrows, pigeons and\npeacocks eat nuts, grains and seeds.\nThey have short, hard, pointed and\nhorny beaks to break the food into\nsmall pieces.\nSparrow Pigeon\nShort, Hard and Horny Beaks\n20EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Can all birds fly ? Name any\nthree which cannot fly.\n2. Can all birds swim ? Name\nany three which can swim.\nFlamingos stand on both legs while they feed.\nWhen they rest, they stand on one leg\nbecause this helps them keep their balance\nbetter.\n\nCurved Beak\nA parrot has a curved beak. It helps it to crack nuts and\nhard fruits. The curved beak also helps it to climb.\nBroad and Flat Beak\nA duck has broad and flat beak. It has tiny holes called\nshovels strainers at the sides and fine holes called all\nalong the edges.\nSuch a beak helps the duck to dig the mud near the\npond, to pick up insects, worms and water plants.\nWater and mud flow out from the strainers and shovels\nleaving insects, worms and water plants in the mouth.\nStrong, Sharp and Hook-shaped Beak\nPreying birds like eagle, vulture, hawk, kite and owl have strong, sharp and hook-\nshaped beaks. This type of beak helps them in tearing the flesh into small pieces.\nCurved Beak of Parrot\nBroad and Flat\nBeak of Duck\nEagle Vulture Kite Owl Hawk\nStrong, Sharp and Hook-shaped Beaks\nBroad, Long and Pointed Beak\nBirds like stork and kingfisher have broad, long\nand pointed beaks. They help them to catch fish\nfrom the water.\nStrong and Heavy Beak\nA woodpecker has a strong and heavy beak. This type of\nbeak is also called . This beak helps it to chisel-shaped beak\npick up food and to make holes in the trunks of the trees.\nStrong and Heavy Beak\nof Woodpecker\nBroad, Long and Pointed\nBeak of Kingfisher\n21EVS-4\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Birds have in place of their hands.____________\na) feathers b) wings\nc) tail d) beak\n2. This bird flies at great heights.\na) Eagle b) Pigeon\nc) Sunbird d) Parrot\n3. It is a flightless bird.\na) Penguin b) Vulture\nc) Parrot d) Eagle\nLong, Thin and Pointed Beak\nA sunbird has a long, thin and pointed beak. It helps the\nbird to suck juice from the fruits and flowers.\nLong and Slender Beak\nA hoopoe has a long and slender beak.\nIt helps it to pull out insects from holes in the ground.Long and Slender\nBeak of Hoopoe\nLong, Thin and Pointed\nBeak of Sunbird\n22EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD There are more than 9000 species of birds in the world.\nD Birds are beautiful animals.\nD Feathers are of different colours in different birds.\nD A bird flaps its wings in both the upward and the downward direction\nto fly.\nD The tail of a bird acts like the rudder of a boat.\nD The claws of a bird help the bird in perching, walking, climbing and\nswimming.\nD Chisel-shaped beak helps to pick up food and to make holes in the\ntrunks of the trees.\n\n4. Birds fly with the help of their .____________\na) feathers b) wings\nc) claws d) none of these\nB. State or :True False\n1. Tail keeps the bird’s body warm.\n2. The tail of a bird acts like the rudder of a boat.\n3. Crow and pigeon fly at great heights.\n4. Eagle and vulture fly only for short distances.\nC. Fill in the blanks :\n1. The feathers cover a bird’s body.______________\n2. Birds body is made up of three parts head, trunk and ._______________\n3. Kiwi is a bird._____________\n4. Aquatic birds have toes to swim in water._____________\nD. Match the following :\n1. Ostrich a) Scratching bird\n2. Sparrow b) Bird of prey\n3. Hen c) Perching bird\n4. Crane d) Flightless bird\n5. Hawk e) Wading bird\nE. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is the most important and unique feature of birds ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What are talons ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. Name the three kinds of feathers. How do they help a bird ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. What are the three sections of the birds’ body ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. Why do different birds have different types of beaks ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n23EVS-4\n\nDo you think a woodpecker will be able to survive if it has thin and a pointed beak ?\nGive reason for your answer.\nDo you know what kinds of birds live in your neighborhood ? Have you ever taken a\nclose look at the birds and observe their behaviour ? In this activity, you will build a\nbird feeder to attract birds, so you can study them :\nMaterials\nD Plastic milk/juice container with cap D Scissors or craft knife\nD Wooden dowel, twig or wooden coffee stirrer\nD Bird seeds D Mini cup(s) D Water\nD Tape D Optional : paper plate\nD Possible decorations : tree bark, shells, stones,\nstickers, etc.\nD Optional : paintbrushes, when using paint\nD Glue or glue gun D Pen or pencil\nFollow the steps :\n1. With the help of an adult, cut a hole into the flat side of the container. It\nshould be 1 inch above the bottom of the container and three inches in\ndiameter. You can have two such holes on two different sides.\n2. Use tape to cover the sharp edges of the opening so the birds donʼt get\nharmed when coming into your bird feeder.\n3. Cut a small hole below the opening and insert a wooden dowel or stick to\nbuild a perch for the birds. Secure the stick in place with glue.\n4. Glue one or more mini cups onto the floor of your bird feeder or onto the\ninside walls.\n5. Prepare a place for your bird food. You can either put a paper plate into your\nbird feeder or plan to put the seeds directly in the container.\n6. Decorate the feeder with different colours.\n7. Fill the bird feeder with seeds and add water in the mini cup.\n8. Hang the bird feeder on tree or set it on a flat surface at a higher level.\nObserve the birds that come to your bird feeder.\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Experiential Learning)\n24EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nMost of the animals are social in nature. They live and more around in groups to\nprotect each other from harsh weather, to defend themselves against enemies\nand to look for food. They help each other in bringing up their young ones.\nThey feel safe when they are together.\nGroups of Animals\nThe oldest or the strongest member of the group becomes the group leader.\nThe leader warns the other members about danger. Cows, zebras, horses,\nelephants, buffaloes and deer live in groups. Their group is called a .herd\nA group of chimpanzees, gorillas or monkeys is called a .band\nA group of lions is called a . A pride consists of two or more adult lions, pride\nseveral lionesses and their cubs.\nThe dog family that includes wolves, foxes, jackals and coyotes move in .packs\nA Pride of Lions A Band of Monkeys A Herd of Elephants\n4\nAnimal\'s World\n• Groups of Animals • Friendly Animals\n• Shy Animals • Give-and-Take Relationship\n• Ears of Animals • Hair and Fur\n25EVS-4\nThe group of caterpillars is called an army of caterpillars while the group of elks is\ncalled a gang.\n\nFriendly Animals\nInsects such as ants, honeybees and termites live together in groups called\ncolonies. The colony of a honeybees has one queen bee, about a hundred\ndrones (males) and more than thousand worker bees.\nA Colony of AntsA Pack of Wolves\nFriendly Animals\nSome animals are friendly and allow us to go near them or feed them. If we give\nthem food, they will immediately come forward to eat it. Cats, cows and dogs are\ngenerally friendly by nature. Humans have tamed many friendly animals. Most of\nthe farm animals like cows, buffaloes, goats, hens and ducks are also friendly\nwith us.\nDolphins are friendly sea creatures. They live in\ngroups. They learn to play tricks easily and\nentertain people. Shows of dolphins performing\ntricks are very popular in abroad. A Dolphin\nA band of chimpanzees can have from 15 to 80 members.\n26EVS-4\n\nBabies of animals enjoy playing. Kittens love to have a friendly fight.\nSometimes, they roll over or pull each other.\nShy Animals\nThere are many animals who run away when\nsomeone approaches them. They are scared of\npeople. These animals are not familiar with\nhuman beings and may sometimes harm them,\ntoo. Rabbits, lizards, squirrels, rats, deer and\nmost of the birds and insects are shy animals.\nTortoise and snails go into their shells if they\nfeel someone is going to harm them. Shy Animals\nGive-and-Take Relationship\nSome species of animals share special relationship with animals of other\nspecies. They help each other.\nD cattle egretsWhite birds called live near large animals such as cow,\nbuffaloes, elephants and deer. They do so because they eat the insects\nthat associate with the cattle. Egrets often perch on the backs of large\nanimals. If the birds sense any danger, they fly away. This is a warning sign\nfor the deer or cattle.\nD Oxpeckers are small birds that ride on large animals such as oxen, rhinos\nand giraffes and peck the insects off their bodies.\nD remoraA is a small fish which sticks to a shark. It eats small creatures that\nlive on the shark’s body.\n27EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Name three shy animals you find around you :\na) _______________ b) _______________ c) _______________\n2. Name three friendly animals :\na) _______________ b) _______________ c) _______________\n\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Why do animals make group ?\n2. Do all animals live in groups ?\nD Egyptian Plover, a bird that feeds on bits of decaying meat stuck in the\nteeth of crocodile. The plover gets its food and the crocodile gets its teeth\ncleaned.\nD Birds and monkeys make noise to alert other animals, if lions, tigers and\nleopards are coming near.\nD Penguins huddle together to keep themselves and their babies warm.\nCattle Egret A Crocodile and Egyptian\nPlover\nOxpeckers and giraffe\nRemora Fish and Shark\nEars of Animals\nAll animals use their ears to hear sound. Some animals\nhave small ears while some animals have long ears.\nAmongst the animals that have ears, there are some\nanimals with ears that are visible, called .external ears\nMammals, including human beings, have external ears,\none on each side of the head.\nAn elephant has big external ears. It uses them to hear\nand to fan itself.\nDonkeys, rabbits and giraffes also have big ears.\n28EVS-4\n\nWe cannot see the ears of some animals. These\nanimals have . Birds have tiny holes internal ears\non both sides of their head. These are covered\nwith feathers. Snakes and lizards do not have ears.\nThey feel the vibrations on the ground and know\nabout the presence of other animals.\nHair and Fur\nSome animals have hair on their body. Some animals have long hair on their\nskin and some animals have small. Animals like yaks, musk oxen and bears that\nlive in very cold areas have long hair which keep them warm. Animals like cows\nand buffaloes have short hair.\nYakCow Buffalo\nYou will observe that the animals which have external ears also have body hair.\nSuch animals are called .mammals\nAnimals like porcupine and hedgehog have needle like hair on their body. It\nhelps them to protect themselves from their enemies.\nThe different designs on an animals’ skin are due to the presence of different\ncoloured hair.\nA leopard has spots on its body. Tigers have striped coat.\n29EVS-4\nD Although hippopotamus is a big animal, it has small ears.\nD It seems that the snake is dancing to the tune of the snake charmer. But\nactually, it just follows the movement of snake charmer’s bean!\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. A group of elephants is called :\na) society b) locality\nc) colony d) herd\n2. Animals like dogs, cats, cows and goats are animals.______________\na) aggressive b) friendly\nc) unfriendly d) shy\n3. Which of these sticks to a shark ?\na) Remora b) Oxpecker\nc) Egyptian Plover d) Cattle Egrets\nTigerLeopardHedgehogPorcupinePorcupine\n30EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Animals that live in groups have a leader who guides the other group\nmembers.\nD Some animals are shy and some are friendly.\nD Many animals help each other.\nD Animals like elephants, donkeys, rabbits and giraffes have external\nears. Birds have internal ears.\nD Mammals are animals that have external ears and body hair.\nD Some animals do not have ears. They feel the vibrations on the ground\nand know the presence of other animals.\n\n4. An animal that lives in a colony is :\na) ant b) dolphin\nc) cow d) elephant\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. is a small fish that sticks to shark for its food.________________\n2. are friendly sea creatures.________________\n3. An has big external ears.________________\n4. Birds have tiny on both sides of their head.________________\nC. Give reasons for the following :\n1. Tortoise go into their shell when they fell danger near them.\n2. Animals live in groups.\n3. Some animals have hair on their body.\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. How can you say that animals are social in nature ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. Who becomes the leader of the group ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What are shy animals ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. How does the egret riding on a buffalo help the buffalo ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. What are external ears ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Adopt a street dog from your neighbourhood. Give food and water to it and see\nhow it becomes friendly and faithful towards you.\nB. Arctic Animalsʼ Camouflage\nThis activity will help you get to know the arctic animals and learn about how they\ndepend on seasonal comouflage to survive. Students will pick one arctic animal\nand research facts about it.\n(Democratic and Human Values)\n(Scientific Temper)\n31EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nMost animals understand the safety and security that comes with unity. But there few\nanimals that prefer to live alone. Find out from the Internet.\nDivide the class in 4-5 group, assign an animal from the given list :\nD Snowshoe Hare D Peary Caribou\nD Arctic Lemming D Ptarmigan\nD Arctic Fox D Polar Bear\nD Arctic Wolf D Harp Seal\nD Snowy Owl D Beluga Whale\nC. Look at the picture below and write the names of the animals in the relevent\ncolumn :\nIt is rainy season and a stray dog has given birth to puppies, in your colony. They do\nnot have any home to live in, would you build a home for them ? (Democratic and Human Values)\n(Digital Resources)\nAnimals with Fur Animals with Feathers\n(Conceptual Learning)\n32EVS-4\n\nTrees and plants are seen in our home, schools, parks, roadsides and in\nmaidans. They are found in different shapes and sizes. They all have different\ntypes of leaves, flowers and fruits.\nTrees and plants are very important for our environment. They add beauty to\nour surroundings. They give us fiber, medicine flowers, fruits and vegetables.\nWe make houses with the help of the wood. We make cloth with the help of the\ncotton. Wood and cotton come from plants. They purify our air by absorbing\ncarbondioxide and generating oxygen. This oxygen is essential to life. They also\nprovide shade and shelter to many animals and birds.\nParts of Plants\nThe structure of a plant is as interesting\nas the human body.\nA plant can be divided into two parts—\nthe shoot system and the root system.\nThe Shoot System\nThe of a plant is that part of shoot system\nthe plant that grows above the ground.\nIt consists of the following parts of the\nplant :\nD D D Stem Flower Leaf\nD D Fruit Bud\nFruit\nFlower\nLeaf\nRoot\nStem\nBud\nShoot SystemRoot System\nParts of Plant\n33EVS-1\n5\nSystem of Plants\n• Parts of Plants • The Shoot System • The Root System\n• Trees Around Us • Caring for Plants',1),(545,12,'Plants and Geography','Unit 3 Plants and Geography\n\nLet us read about the shoot system of a plant in detail :\nStem\nThe part of a plant just above the ground is called the . The stem of a tree stem\nis the strongest part of the tree and is known as .trunk\nThe stem performs many important functions :\nD It supports all the parts of the plant.\nD It carries water and minerals from the roots to the leaves.\nD It carries prepared food from the leaves to other parts of the plant.\nD It stores food in some plants. Stems of these plants grow below the\nground. These stems are called .underground modified stems\nUnderground Modified Stems\nPotato\nScale\nleaf\nBud\nEye\nGinger\nScale leaf\nBud\nLeaf\nLeaves are thin, flat and green part of a plant.\nWe can identify a plant by its leaves because\nthe leaves of different plants are different.\nLeaves are known as the Kitchen of the Plant\nbecause the food is made here. A leaf has\ngreen pigment called . The leaves chlorophyll\nmake food in the presence of sunlight, water, air and minerals. This process is\ncalled .photosynthesis\nOxygen\nCarbon\ndioxide\nWater\nFood travels to other parts\nSun\nPhotosynthesis\n34EVS-1\nSome shallowly rooted trees found in the rainforests have roots growing on all the\nsides. Such roots are called buttress roots. They prevent the trees from falling and\ngather more nutrients for the tree.\n\nBud\nBud is a baby flower. It grows into a beautiful flower.\nBud\nFlower\nFlower is the most beautiful part of a plant. Some flowers\nhave sweet smell.\nRose\nFruit\nFruit is the delicious part of a plant. Some fruits\nare eaten raw, while some fruits called\nvegetables are eaten after cooking.\nFruits and Vegetables\nThe Root System\nThe is usually found underground. In some plants, roots also grow root system\nabove the ground. They keep the plants erect at one place. They absorb water\nand minerals from the soil. They also check soil erosion.\nThere are four different types of roots :\nTap Root\nLike carrot and radish, tap roots grow long\nand thick into the soil. Tiny hair-like roots\ngrow out of the main root to absorb water\nand minerals.\nFibrous Root\nLike grass and rice the primary root dies at\nan early stage and numerous roots grow\nfrom the lower part of the stem. These roots\nappear as a bunch of fibres as many small\nroots grow sideways.\nTap Root\nFibrous Root\n35EVS-1\n\nStorage Root\nThe vegetables like carrot, turnip, tapioca, etc., are storage roots. They store\nfood prepared by the leaves and are rich sources of energy.\nRadish Turnip Carrot Sweet potato\nStorage Roots\nAerial Roots\nThese roots grow out of the branches of huge\ntrees. They grow towards the soil and support the\ntree. For example, banyan tree.\nAerial roots of Banyan Tree\nFlowering trees, like cherry and\nmuskogee are pleasant to look at.\nCherry Muskogee\nWe also see common trees like\neucalyptus and banyan around us.\nEucalyptus Banyan\nIn an orchard, we see fruit trees\nlike apple and pineapple.\nApple Pineapple\nTrees Around Us\nThere are different kinds of trees surrounding us.\n36EVS-1\nBamboo is the fastest growing\nplant. One of its species can\ngrow up to 35 inches per day.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Have you ever grown a plant ?\n2. How should we take care of the\nplants ?\n3. Have you ever taken any vegetable\nor fruit directly from a farm ?\n4. Why some plants are kept inside the\nhouse ?\n\nIn the cold hilly areas,\nwe find trees like fir and pine.\nTrees like teak and shisham\nprovide us with wood.\nFir Pine Teak Shisham\nDo not cut trees\nCaring for Plants\nWe should take proper care of plants as they\nare very useful to us. We should not cut down\ntrees. We should not allow anyone to cut down\na tree. Cutting trees will lead to serious\nproblems like pollution, drought and flooding.\nThere are many Government agencies that\ntake care of trees in their area of work. These\nagencies employ gardeners to take care of trees. They plant trees along road\nsides and other suitable places. They water them regularly.\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Plants and trees are very important for our .______________\na) roads b) houses c) environment d) none of these\n37EVS-1\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Plants are very important for our environment.\nD A plant has mainly two parts—the shoot system and the root system.\nD The shoot system is the part of a plant which is above the ground.\nD The root system is the part of a plant which is under the soil.\nD Leaf is known as the ‘Kitchen of the Plant’.\nD We should not cut down trees.\n\n2. is the kitchen of a plant.________\na) Root b) Stem\nc) Leaf d) Bud\n3. The part of the plant just above the ground is called ._________\na) stem b) leaf\nc) flower d) fruit\n4. is not a part of the shoot system.________\na) Root b) Bud\nc) Flower d) stem\nB. Name two plants which :\n1. Give us wood for furniture _____________ ____________\n2. Have tap roots _____________ ____________\n_____________ ____________3. Have fibrous roots\n_____________ ____________4. Store food in their leaves\nC. Match the following :\n1. The strongest part of a tree a) food maker of the plant\n2. Leaf b) rice\n3. Tap root c) banyan tree\n4. Fibrous root d) stem\n5. Aerial root e) carrot\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. How plants are important for us and our environment ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What does the shoot system consist of ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What is root system ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. Write the main functions of leaves.\n____________________________________________________________________\n38EVS-1\n\nA. Collect pictures of various leaves, fruits, vegetables, flowers and buds. Paste them\nand name them in your scrapbook.\nB. Name the following :\n1. Part of the plant that grows above ground ___________________\n2. Roots that grow out of branches of large tree ___________________\n3. Most beautiful part of a plant ___________________\n4. Process of making food by the plants ___________________\n5. Kitchen of the plant ___________________\nC. Hydroponics\nPlants can grow without soil, too, using a process called hydroponics. In this\nactivity, you will transform a two liter water bottle into a mini-garden that needs\nonly light, air, and nutrient-rich water to grow. In this activity, we will try to learn\nhydroponics—the process of growing plants in nutrient-rich water.\nNutrients are not found in pure water. We need to add them.\n1. Measure 1 quarter filtered, or purified water into a container. Avoid\nunfiltered tap water. Mix that into the water in your second bottle or\ncontainer as per the instruction given on the bottle.\n2. Place three seeds in the coconut coir just above the knot in the wick.\n3. Cover the seeds with the coir.\n4. Pour the prepared water over the growing medium. It will seep into the\nreservoir. Keep pouring until the reservoir is about 1/2 to 2/3 full. The water\nshould never reach the spout of the bottle. Make sure that the area around\nthe seeds or tiny plant gets wet as you pour the water.\n5. Once the seed is sprouted, place the container near a window as now the\nplant needs air and sunlight too.\n6. Check daily to make sure the area around the seed or tiny plant is moist. Add\na little water if the level goes down. As the plant grows, its roots will extend\ndeeper and get better access to the water and nutrients sucked up by the\nwick.\nHow do honeybees make honey ? What do you mean by apiculture ?\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Scientific Temper)\n(Art Integration)\n(Critical Thinking)\n39EVS-1\nTASKS\n\nPlants are of two types—flowering and non-flowering. Some flowering plants\nare rose, jasmine, sunflower, lotus, tulip and daisy.\nFlowers are beautiful and the most attractive parts of a plant. There are\ndifferent kinds of flowers. They are of different shapes, sizes and colours.\nFlowers grow from buds. When buds open, petals spread out to form flowers.\nParts of a Flower\nPetal\nStigma\nStyleAnther\nFilament\nOvule Ovary\nSepal\nParts of a Flower\nA flower has different parts as shown in the picture.\nPlants reproduce through flowers. The colourful\npetals attract insects like butterflies, etc. They\ncollect pollen from the stamen as food. Then they\ntransfer this pollen to the pistil of another flower to\nwhich they go. This process is called . It pollination\nhelps to produce seeds.\nFunctions of a Flower\nSeeds are found in fruits. Flowers wither and change into fruits. Fruits have\nseeds inside them. When conditions are favourable, seeds germinate and grow\ninto new plants.\nNew plant grows from a seed\nFlower Fruit Seed New Plant\n6\nValley of Flowers\n40EVS-4\n• Parts of a Flower • Functions of Flowers\n• Flowering and Non-flowering Plants • Uses of Flowers\n• How do we get Flowers ?\n\nFlowering Plants\nDifferent types of plants bear flowers in\ndifferent seasons. There are different\nflowers in different seasons. There are\nsome flowering plants which bear flowers\nthroughout the year.\nSunflower Tulip\nMarigold Rose\nFlowering Plants in Spring Flowering Plants in Autumn\nJasmine Orchid\nDahlia Sweet pea\nFlowering Plants in SummerFlowering Plants in Winter\nNon-flowering Plants\nAll some plants do not bear flowers. Such plants are called . non-flowering plants\nMoss, mushroom, fern, mould, etc., are some examples of non-flowering plants.\nMushroomMoss Fern\nUses of Flowers\nA flower sometimes known as a . Flowers are used in various bloom blossomor\nways.\nTitan arum is the world’s tallest\nflower, about 2-5 metre tall. It\ngrows in the forests of Sumatra.\n41EVS-4\n\nLet us read about some uses of flowers :\nD Flowers are used for worship.\nD Flowers are used to make perfume.\nD Women wear flowers in their hair as gajra.\nD Some flowers are used as herbal medicines.\nD Flowers are used to decorate houses, offices, etc.\nD Gulkand is made from rose flower.\nD Honeybees collect nectar from flowers to make honey.\nD Garlands of flowers are used in various ceremonies.\nHow do we get Flowers ?\nMany people plant flowers around their houses. We have many gardens in our\ncities which are maintained by the governments.\nFlowers are also sold in markets. People who sell flowers are called .florists\nThese flowers are brought from different cities. Some flowers are imported\nfrom other countries as well. Florists usually sell bouquets.\nGajra\nA Flower ShopA Flower Garden A Florist\nFlowers make our world a beautiful place. They bring joy and happiness to our\nlives. Do not pluck flowers. They look nice in the garden.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Which is the most beautiful part of the plant ?\n2. What are two different types of plants ?\n42EVS-4\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Rose, jasmine, lotus are examples of plants.______________\na) flowering b) non-flowering\nc) both a) & b) d) none of these\n2. Plants reproduce through .______________\na) stem b) flowers\nc) leaves d) roots\n3. is an example of non-flowering plant.______________\na) Mushroom b) Mango\nc) Rose d) Mint\nB. Write or :True False\n1. Seeds are found in fruits.\n2. Flowers grow from the fruits.\n3. All plants bear flowers.\n4. The colourful petals attract insects like butterflies, etc.\nC. Fill in the blanks :\n1. are found inside the fruits.___________\n2. Orchid bears flowers in season. ___________\n3. germinate and grow into new plants.___________\n4. Some flowers are used as herbal .______________\nD. Write the seasons of the following plants :\n1. Rose : _____________________\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD A flower has different parts.\nD A plant can be a flowering plant or a non-flowering plant.\nD Flowers grow from buds.\nD Flowers are the reproductive part of a plant.\nD Insects collect pollen from the stamen and transfer it to the pistil.\n43EVS-4\n\n2. Tulip : _____________________\n3. Zinnia : _____________________\n4. Sunflower : _____________________\nE. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is the importance of flowers in our life ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What is pollination ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What are the different parts of a flower ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Collect pictures of your favourite flowers and paste them in your scrapbook.\nB. Visit a garden and make a list of flowers that you see there. Write their names and\nthe season when they grow in your scrapbook.\nC. Find out the names of flowers that are used for :\n1. Oils : ____________________ ____________________\n2. Perfumes : ____________________ ____________________\n3. Essence : ____________________ ____________________\n4. Face Pack : ____________________ ____________________\nD. Label the parts of a flower in the given boxes :\nHow many species of flowering plants are there in India ?\n(Art Integration)\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Conceptual Learning)\n(Knowledge of India)\nTASKS\n44EVS-4\n\nAll work and no-play makes our mind and body dull.\nGames are fun. Games keep us active and healthy. Games make our mind\nsharp. Games help us learning teamwork.\nGames Played At School\nChildren enjoy playing games in the classroom. Classroom games are generally\nrelated to learning. Crossword, dumb charades, quiz, etc., are\nsome common games that children play in the classroom.\nThese games are either played in\nsmall groups or they involve the\nwhole class.\nDuring the games period, children\nplay different outdoor games like\ncricket, badminton, basketball,\nfootball and kho-kho. They need\ntraining to play these games.\n45EVS-4\n7\nRecreation\n• Games Played at School • Play Fair Games\n• Games Played at Home • Visiting Fair • Visiting Circus\nRAPIDTASK\n1. What games do you play at your school ?\n2. What are the indoor games that you play with your family members ?\nThe first sports as we know it today was most likely wrestling, which originated in\nGreece.',1),(546,13,'Culture and Traditions','Unit 4 Culture and Traditions\n\nExperts of the games train children how to play these games. The school\nprovides all the things needed for these games. The children return these\nplaythings to the sports teacher when the games period is over.\nPlay Fair Games\nChildren get into arguments and fight while\nplaying. If such a thing happens, you should\ntake the help of a teacher or an adult to\nsettle the issue. The best way of solving\nquarrels is to follow the rules of the games\nstrictly. Even grown ups get into arguments\nwhile playing. Each sport has an umpire or a\nreferee to see that the game is played fairly\nand according to the rules. A good sport person has sportsman’s spirit which\nhelps him/her to overcome all obstacles.\nGames Played at Home\nAt home, children play both indoor and outdoor games. They play with their\nfamily members, friends and neighbours.\nLudo, chess, carrom, etc., are some indoor games played by the children.\nNow-a-days, children love to play games on\ncomputers and mobiles. Children play outdoor\ngames in parks and playgrounds in the\nevening. They play cricket, football,\nhide and seek and many other games in\nthe park.\nCricket\nKite Flying\nKite Flying\nIn olden days, people use to recreate\nthemselves with sports, like kite flying,\nspinning tops, playing marbles and\ngames like kabaddi and kho-kho.\n46EVS-4\n\nSpinning Tops\nTop lattu or is a pear-shaped toy usually made of wood. A\ntop gets into motion by pulling a string that has been\nwrapped around it. Children enjoy seeing the spinning top.\nVisiting Fair\nFairs are one of the most enjoyable means of recreation\nand enjoyment.\nBig fairs are organized on the occasion of festivals like\nDussehra, Diwali, Christmas, New Year, etc.\nThere are many stalls at a fair. People enjoy eating different kinds of food here.\nChildren love to play games at different stalls. There are many games where\nthey win prizes, too. There are many swings, slides and other rides for children\nto enjoy in the fair. It is a good means of recreation.\nVisiting Circus\nCircus is a group of performers who travel from one place to another to\nentertain people. Acrobats, jugglers, trapeze artists, tightrope walkers and\nclowns amuse the audience by their amazing acts. Many animals are trained to\nentertain the people.\nClowns or jokers dress up in funny costumes and do make-up to entertain the\naudience with their humorous acts.\nCircus\n47EVS-4\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. All works and no play makes us .______________\na) activity b) smart c) healthy d) dull\n2. Big fairs are organised on the occasions of .______________\na) exams b) festivals\nc) pandemic d) none of these\n3. The shape of a top is :\na) apple like b) pear like c) diamond like d) none of these\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. We play games to our mind.________________\n2. and are some common classroom games.________________ ______________\n3. Each sport has its own set of and .________________ ________________\n4. Children play both and games.________________ ________________\n5. There are many games available on and also._____________ _____________\n48EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Have you ever visited a circus ? How was your experience ?\n2. Which National festival is associated with Kite flying ?\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD We have fun when we do things to enjoy. This is called recreation.\nD Children enjoy playing different games.\nD We must follow the rules of the games.\nD A good sportsperson has sportsman’s spirit.\nD Children love to play with their neighbourhood friends.\nD In olden days, people used to recreate themselves with sports like kite\nflying, spinning tops, playing marbles and games like Kabaddi and\nkho-kho, etc.\nD Children have fun at fairs and at a circus.\n\nC. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is recreation ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. Why should we play games ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. Which games are better indoor or outdoor ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. Why should we play a game according to its rules ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n5. What is a fair ? Write two lines on any fair that you have visited.\n____________________________________________________________________\nList the activities in appropriate columns :\nCamping Dancing Net Surfing Enjoying at Beach\nWatching TV Fishing Listening to Music Reading Book\nShopping Flying Kite\nIndoor Leisure Activities Outdoor Leisure Activities\n________________________________ ________________________________\n________________________________ ________________________________\n________________________________ ________________________________\n________________________________ ________________________________\n________________________________ ________________________________\nTalk to your friend.\n• What do you like to do in your free time ?\n• How often do you do it ?\n• With whom do you share that recreational activity ?\n• Name an activity which you donʼt like to do.\nWhy the circuses are losing their popularity day by day ?\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Communication Skills)\n(Critical Thinking)\n49EVS-4\nTASKS\n\n50EVS-4\n8 Safety and\nFirst-Aid\n• Safety • First-Aid\nSafety\nWhen we work, study or play, we move or handle many things. Thus, we can get\nharmed due to the various types of movements.\nFollowing are some safety rules that should be followed by all to avoid the\naccidents :\nDo not disturb the\nbus driver.\nDo not jump on\nthe desks.\nAlways stand in a queue at\nthe bus stop.\nUse zebra crossing while\ncrossing the road.\nDo not get in or out of a\nmoving bus.\nDo not lean out of the\nmoving bus.',1),(547,14,'Caring for self and others','Unit 5 Caring for self and others\n\nAvoid using blades to\nsharpen pencils.\nDo not keep toys on\nthe floor.\nKeep your things at the\nright place.\n51EVS-4\nD o not go alone for\nswimming. D o not touch electric wires. D o not run on the stairs.\nFirst-aid\nAn immediate medical aid before an injured person is shifted to the hospital for\naccurate and treatment is called . Timely help can save the diagnosis first-aid\nlife of the injured person. First-aid is given for minor , nose bleeding wounds\nand minor burns. It is not an alternative of complete medical treatment. Any\nperson can give first-aid but he must get basic training to do so.\nFollowing is a list of first-aid given in different kinds of injuries :\nSprain\nThe twisting and swelling up of joints, such as the\nankle or wrist results in sprain. The tissues around\nthe twisted joint are damaged and causes pain.\nv The sprained part should not be moved\nbefore it is healed.\n\n52EVS-4\nv For first-aid, a cold ice-pack should be used on the affected area.\nv Apply the ointment on the sprained joint two or three times a day.\nv Wrap the elastic bandage around the sprained joint after applying the\nointment.\nFracture\nA crack or break in bone is called . The fracture\nfractured bone causes a lot of pain. The affected area\nmay swell up and it will not be able to be moved.\nFollowing are first-aid steps in case of fracture :\nv Do not allow the person to move.\nv Tie a or a to give support to the splin slingt\nbroken bone.\nThe fractured arm can be supported by a . It sling\ngives support to the arm and prevents its movement.\nSling\nSplint\nBurns\nBurns are caused due to heat, chemicals etc.\nv In case of minor burns, the affected skin develops a\nreddish colour.\nv Wash the affected area with cold water.\nv After that, apply burnol and place clean cloth on it to prevent any infection.\nv In case of severe burns, are formed. The blisters must never be blisters\npricked with a pin or any other sharp object. It may lead to infection.\nv Instead, a wet cloth piece dipped in a solution of baking soda can be\napplied on the burnt area.\nv In case, if clothes of a person catches fire roll up the person in a blanket.\nv Take the victim to the doctor immediately after the first-aid is administered.\nv If it is a chemical burn, the burnt area must be washed with lots of water to\nrinse away the chemical and then the victim must be taken to the doctor\nimmediately.\n\nMinor Cuts And Wounds\nThe wound should be cleaned with soap and water and\nthen with cotton soaked in dettol or savlon. If there is\nbleeding from the wound, ice will stop or minimize the\nflow of blood. Apply an antiseptic ointment, cover with\ngauze and tie a bandage.\nDeep Wounds\nIn case of deep wound, it should be cleaned first with\ncotton dipped in an antiseptic solution.\nv Bleeding must be stopped by tying a bandage tightly\nabove the wound.\nAnother way to reduce the bleeding is always to apply a thick pad of cotton\nover the wound and press it with the thumb and fingers.\nNose Bleeding\nFollowing steps should be taken to give first aid in\ncase of nose bleeding :\nv Make the patient sit on a chair comfortably. Tilt his head\nback and ask him to keep his arms folded above his head.\nv Prepare an ice pack and keep on the patient’s\nnose. It will reduce bleeding.\nv Ask the patient to breathe through his mouth.\nv Take the patient to hospital if there is heavy bleeding.\nFires\nFire can be caused because of unsafe use of match sticks, candles, kerosene\nand fire crackers. Gas leakage in kitchen, electrical faults etc.\n53EVS-4\nv Tetanus is a disease caused when the skin is cut by a dirty or rusted object.\nv Anti-tetanus injection given within 24 hours of the accident prevents the\ninfection from spreading.\n\nFollowing are some safety rules to avoid fire break out :\nv Electrical wiring should also be checked regularly.\nv Keep petrol and kerosene oil in air-tight container\nand away from fire.\nv Avoid wearing synthetic clothes while working in\nthe kitchen.\nv Never forget to switch off the gas cylinder after\nuse.\nv Handle the match sticks, candles and crackers carefully.\nPutting out fires\nFire can be put out in the following ways :\nv Fire are devices to put out fires. extinguishers\nThey contain carbon dioxide (CO ) gas or 2\nfoam. The gas surrounds the fire and puts it off\nby cutting off the air supply.\nv Throw sand or mud over the fire caused by\npetrol.\nv For electric fire, switch off the mains immediately. Throw sand on the fire.\nDon’t throw water over a fire caused by electricity.\nv In case of big fire, call fire brigade by dialing ‘101’.\nPoisoning\nIn case of poisoning, do the following :\nv Try to make the vomit.victim\nv Rush the victim to a doctor.\n54EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. We should keep petrol in _______________.\n2. Fire can be caused due to _______________ and _______________.\n3. We can call fire brigade by dialing _______________.\n\nAnimal Bites\nThe saliva of some animals contains viruses. For example, dogs, cats, monkeys,\nwolves and jackals. The viruses enter the victim’s body\nthrough the animal’s saliva when these animals bite\nsomeone and they cause rabies to the victim.\nv Wash the wound with soap and water to wash away\ngerms.\nv Apply an antiseptic on the wound. Bandage the wound\nwith a sterile gauze to prevent the infection.\nv Take the victim to the doctor immediately. The doctor\nmay give anti-rabies injections.\nIn case of snake bite :\nv To stop the flow of poison along with blood to the heart\nand brain, apply a tight bandage just above the bite.\nv Keep the victim still movement will cause the poison to\nspread faster in the victim’s body.\nv The victim should be immediately taken to the doctor.\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option.3\n1. Burns are caused due to :\na) heat b) chemicals c) both a) & b)\n2. Which is the good conductor of electricity ?\na) Mud b) Sand c) Water\n55EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Accidents happen due to carelessness.\nD we should try to avoid accidents by following safety rules.\nD First-aid is the immediate help given to injured person.\nD Different kinds of injuries need different kinds of first-aid.\n\n3. Which is the common problem among children during summer ?\na) Sprain b) Wound c) Nose bleeding\n.4 How can you put off fire caused due to electrical fault ?\na) By Water b) By fire extinguisher c) By sand or mud\n5. A cloth sling can be used in case of :\na) burns b) fractures c) poisoning\nB. Write for true and for false statements.T F\n1. In case of a fracture, it must be ensured that the fractured part is not\nmoved.\n2. Sprain is caused due to crack in bone.\n3. We should wash our hands before giving first-aid to an injured person.\n4. The saliva of animals such as dogs and cats cause a dangerous\ndisease called rabies.\n5. Naphthalene balls are also poisonous.\n6. We should move a person with snake bite.\nC. Fill in the blanks.\n1. The saliva of animals contains . _____________\n2 , _____________. In case of small burns apply on the burnt area.\n3. _____________Any break or crack in the is called fracture.\n_____________.4. The immediate help given to an injured person is called\n5 _____________. If an animal bites, the doctor may give injections.\n6. Fire due to petrol can be extinguished by ______________.\nD. Answer the following questions.\n1. What is first-aid ?\n2. How would you help someone who has nose bleeding ?\n3. Name any three causes of fire.\n4. How would you help if a person get a deep wounds ?\n5. How would you help the person with snake bite ?\n6. What is fracture ? What first-aid should you give to an injured person ?\n56EVS-4\n\nA. Name the type of injury below each picture for which the first aid is being\nfollowed.\n_______________________ _______________________\nB. List some of the common accidents that can happen in the following places.\nPlace Accident\nIn school\nAt home\nOn road\nC. Visit a fire station with your friends and teacher. See there how a fire brigade\nworks and how they get to fire sites ? What equipments do they have ? Make a\nreport on the fire station in your scrapbook.\nD. Use your imagination and make a poster which shows that a little carefulness can\navoid an accident.\nE. A number of accidents on the road happens due to carelessness and not\nfollowing the rules. Accidents can also occur at home, in school and on any\npublic place due to carelessness of ourselves and some other person. We should\ntry to avoid them by following the safety rules.\n57EVS-4\nTASKS\n(Conceptual Learning)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Art Integration)\n(Life Skills)\n\nHere are some activities shown in the pictures. Write for the activities that yes\nshould be done and for the activities that should not be done.no\nv Salon is a four year old girl. She is very naughty. She wants to help her mother in i\nthe kitchen. But her mother does not allow her to come in the kitchen. Why ?\nv Why should we not operate any electrical switches when there is a gas leak ?\n58EVS-4\n\nFood is our basic need. We cannot live without food.\nWe need food for the following reasons :\nD Food gives us energy to work.\nD It protects us from diseases.\nD It keeps us healthy and active.\nFood from Plants\nMost of the food that we eat comes from the plants. Cereals, pulses, fruits,\nvegetables, spices, oil, etc., are all obtained from plants. We also get beverages\nlike tea and coffee from the plants.\nCereals and Pulses\nFood grains like rice, wheat, oats, maize and millets are called . Urad, cereals\ngram, arhar, kidney beans, etc., are pulses.\nCereals and pulses together are called food grains. Rice is a staple food in many\nparts of the country. Wheat flour is used to make chapatis, bread, etc.\nWheat Rice Maize Pulses Gram\nFruits and Vegetables\nFruits and vegetables are also obtained from the plants. They are full of vitamins\n9\nFood We Eat\n• Food from Plants • The Journey of Food\n• Food of Different Regions\n59EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. How the process of growing\ncrops begin ?\n2. Who prepares tasty food for you\nat home ?',1),(548,15,'Food','Unit 6 Food\n\nand minerals that are good for health. They protect us from diseases and keep\nus healthy. Mango, banana, cherry, orange, pineapple, papaya and grapes are\nsome popular fruits.\nFruits are eaten raw. We also drink the juices of different fruits. Peas, carrot,\nbottle gourd, potato, turnip and radish are some common vegetables. They are\neaten raw or after cooking.\nFruits Vegetables\nSpices\nCurry\nLeaves\nChillies\nCloves\nCinnamon\nCardamom\nBlack Pepper Turmeric\nSpices\nSpices are added to the food to give\nit flavour, taste and colour. We get\nspices from flowers, stem, fruits,\nbark, seeds and roots of the plants.\nOilseeds\nDifferent kinds of oils are used in different parts of\nIndia for cooking. Oil is extracted from special\nseeds called . Oilseeds are crushed to get oilseeds\noil. Groundnut, sesame, mustard, olive, sunflower\nand soyabean are some of the most commonly\nused oilseeds.\n60EVS-4\nRaisins, almonds, cashewnuts, pistachio and walnuts are some of the dry fruits we\neat.\n\nPloughing the Fields Sowing the Seeds Irrigating the Fields\nBeverages\nLeaves of some plants are used to make drinks. These drinks are called\nbeverages. Tea and coffee are popular beverages obtained from plants.\nThe Journey of Food\nThe journey of food starts with farmers,\nfishermen and dairy workers. Farmers grow\ncrop plants on large areas of land called\nfarms. The farmer first prepares the field\nby it. Small farmers use oxen to ploughing\nplough their fields, whereas big farmers\nuse tractors for the same.\nThen, he adds and to the soil to increase the yield of his manures fertilizers\ncrop. The second step is the seeds. After a few days, the seeds sowing\ngerminate and small plants appear.\nThe third step is the crops. The young plants are watered regularly irrigating\nand sprayed with pesticides and insecticides to keep them safe from insects.\nFarmer Grows Crops\n61EVS-4\nSpices like ginger, cloves and turmeric can be used as medicines.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. What we get from plants ?\n2. Which fruit do you like the most ?\n\nThe crops grow in about six months. They are then ready to be cut. The fourth\nstep is , i.e., cutting the crops.harvesting\nAfter the crops have been harvested, grains are separated from the plants. This\nis called . Removing the husk further cleans grains. This is called threshing\nwinnowing. Now, the crops are ready to be packed and sent to the market on\ntractor, truck or bullock-cart.\nHarvesting the Crop Threshing the Crop Winnowing the Crop\nThe crop is transported to the wholesale market or mandi. From the wholesale\nmarket, this food stuff is bought by the retailers. We buy the food stuff from the\nretailers.\nWholesalers buy and transport\nfood items from the fields to the\nwholesale markets.\nFarmers grow crops\nin their fields.\nRetailers and vendors buy\nfood items from mandis\nand sell them in local\nmarkets.\nThe food items are washed,\ncleaned and cooked at\nhome.\nWe eat and enjoy this\nfood together.\n62EVS-4\n\nFood of Different Regions\nDal-Bati\nChoorma from\nRajasthan\nMakki-Ki-Roti,\nSarson-Ka-Saag\nfrom Punjab\nFish Curry, Rice\nand Rasogulla\nfrom Bengal\nDhokla, Thepla\nand Khandvi from\nGujarat\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Which of these is not a grain ?\na) Wheat b) Rice\nc) Maize d) Clove\n2. Farmers start their farming activity with .____________\na) adding fertilizers b) irrigation\nc) ploughing d) sowing\n63EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Food is one of our most basic needs.\nD Plants are the main source of food.\nD Cereals and pulses together are called food grains.\nD Oil is extracted from special seeds called oilseeds.\nD Fruits and vegetables are also obtained from plants.\nD Spices are added to the food to give its flavour, taste and colour.\nD The journey of food has these steps :\n1. Ploughing the field 2. Sowing the seeds 3. Irrigating the field\n4. Harvesting the crop 5. Threshing the crop 6. Winnowing the crop\nD Different regions have their own special food items.\n\n3. Cardamom is a :\na) fruit b) oilseed\nc) spice d) pulses\n4. Wholesalers sell food to the :\na) retailers b) shopkeepers\nc) both a) & b) d) none of these\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. Most of the food that we eat comes from ._____________\n2. , , and are dry fruits.____________ ____________ ____________ ____________\n3. Manure is added to the soil to increase the ._____________\n4. Separating grains from the husk is called . _____________\nC. Give two examples of each :\n1. Fruits : _______________________ _______________________\n2. Vegetables : _______________________ _______________________\n3. Spices : _______________________ _______________________\n4. Beverages : _______________________ _______________________\nD. Write the journey of food in your own words in the space provided below :\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\nE. What are beverages ? Give some examples also :\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\n_________________________________________________________________________\nF. Answer the following questions :\n1. Can we live without food ? Why / Why not ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n64EVS-4\n\n2. What are food grains ? Explain it.\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What are beverages ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. What are oilseeds and how do we get oil from them ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n65EVS-4\nTASKS\n(Art Integration)\nMilk Rice\nFish\nYogurt\nBroccoliBeens\nBread\nBanana\nA. Make a Food Group Wheel on a card paper as shown below :\nCut a hole in the centre of the wheel. Take a spinner. Cut a small arrow shape on a\npaper. Paste it on one at the legs of the spinner. Now adjust the spinner in the\ncentre hole on the wheel. Spin the spinner and wait for it to stop determine the\nfood group of the food item in the direction of the arrow.\n\nB. Collect the samples of different spices in small plastic packets. Attach them in\nyour EVS notebook and label them.\nC. Collect an empty wrapper of any breakfast cereal. It can be oats, cornflakes,\nporridge or any other. Read the information on the wrapper and fill in the blanks :\n1. Name of the product : ________________________________________\n2. Name of the brand : ________________________________________\n3. Quantity : ________________________________________\n4. Rate : ________________________________________\n5. Content : ________________________________________\n6. Date of packing : ________________________________________\n7. Date of expiry : ________________________________________\nD. Write the names of the states of India to their correct famous food :\n1. 2. 3. 4.\n(Art Integration)\n(Critical Thinking)\n(Knowledge of India)\nWe eat food to remain healthy. How should we ensure that we eat healthy food ?\nAt times, when we are not hungry, we waste food. But, there are so many people who\ndonʼt get any food to eat. How can you, as a student, help to solve this problem ?\n(Scientific Temper)\n(Problem Solving)\n66EVS-4\n\nWater is the basic need of all living organism. It includes human beings animals\nand plants. We all need clean water to drink and for other needs.\nAbout 97% of the Earth’s water is stored in sea and oceans. This water is salty\nand cannot be used for drinking only 3% is fresh water which we can drink.\nWater Pollution\nHarmful chemical wastes from homes and factories, fertilizers, pesticides,\nsewage and dead bodies make the water impure and unfit for drinking. This is\ncalled and the substances which cause pollution are called water pollution\npollutants.\nCauses of Water Pollution\nD Throwing dead bodies in water.\nD Oil spilled by ships and tankers.\nD Discharge of industrial wastes in water.\nD Washing clothes on the river banks.\nD Discharge of domestic wastes in water bodies.\nEffects of Water Pollution\nWhen polluted water is used by animals or human beings directly or indirectly —\nD It can cause diseases like typhoid, cholera, jaundice, diarrhoea, etc.\nD Aquatic animals become sick and die in polluted water.\nD Polluted water makes soil infertile and crops get damaged.\n10\nWater ‘O’ Water\n• Water Pollution • Causes of Water Pollution\n• Effects of Water Pollution • Every Drop Counts\n• Purification of Water\n67EVS-4',1),(549,16,'Water','Unit 7 Water\n\nD Water plants get infected and die in\npolluted water.\nD When infected aquatic animals are eaten\nby human beings or other animals, it\nmakes them sick and unhealthy.\nWe must check water pollution if we want to\nimprove the quality of water bodies. Water\npollution should be controlled because there is limited amount of fresh water\npresent on our Earth.\nEvery Drop Counts\nWater is precious. Although 70% of our Earth is covered with water, still\nall this water cannot be used for daily activities as\nit is saline. Fresh water is available in the form of\nunderground water, water in rivers, ponds and\nlakes. This is not sufficient for everyone. This is the\ncause of water shortage.\nThus, we need to be a little careful to conserve\nwater. Rainwater harvesting is a very good method\nto conserve rainwater and store it for later use. This is done in open in rural areas.\nRainwater Harvesting\nPurification of Water\nWater that is pure for drinking is called . Water from rivers, lakes, potable water\nreservoirs and other water bodies is brought through channels to the water-\ntreatment plant. Here, this water is made pure for drinking. At the water-\ntreatment plant, water is first filtered to remove the impurities. It is then treated\nwith chemicals to kill the germs present in it. This purified water is then\npumped into huge water tanks. From here, the water reaches to the taps in our\n68EVS-4\nFrozen water is slightly lighter than liquid water. That’s why we see ice floating on\nwater.\n\nhouses through a network of\npipelines. The height of the\ntank gives steady pressure\nto water passing through the\npipes to our taps.\nWater can be made safe at\nhome by the following\nmethods :\nBoiling\nBoiling kills the germs in water. Water should be\nboiled and cooled before drinking.\nUsing Chemicals\nChlorine tablets are added to water to kill germs in\nit. This process is called .chlorination\nFiltration\nSolid impurities like dirt and sand can be removed by filtering water. Now-a-\ndays, ultraviolet purifiers are used to make water pure for drinking.\nPeople use purifiers or Reverse Osmosis system to kill germs in water.\nWater Treatment Plant\n69EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Harmful chemical wastes from homes and factories, fertilizers,\npesticides, sewage and dead bodies pollute the water.\nD Polluted water disturbs the aquatic life by killing fish and water plants.\nD Polluted water can cause diseases like jaundice, typhoid, dysentery,\ndiarrhoea and cholera.\nD Water which is pure for drinking is called potable water.\nD We should be a little careful to conserve water.\nD We must make sure that the water we drink is clean.\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Water is the basic need of all organism.__________\na) living b) non-living\nc) thirsty d) none of these\n2. Which of these is a cause of water pollution ?\na) Bathing animals b) lmmersion of idols\nc) Dumping of wastes d) All of these\n3. About of Earth’s surface is covered with water.__________\na) 50% b) 60% c) 70% d) 80%\n4. How can we clean water ?\na) By boiling b) By adding chlorine tablets\nc) Both a) & b) d) None of these\nB. Write or :True False\n1. Water fit for drinking is called potable water.\n2. There is no need to cover the drinking water.\n3. Boiling cannot kill the germs present in water.\n4. Purifiers kill the germs present in water.\nC. Fill in the blanks :\n1. Throwing dead bodies in the causes water pollution.________________\n2. Water animals become and die.________________\n3. Pure water that we can drink is called water.________________\nD. Explain each in two sentences :\n1. Chlorination : ______________________________________________________\n___________________________________________________________________\n2. Filtration : __________________________________________________________\n___________________________________________________________________\n3. Boiling : ___________________________________________________________\n___________________________________________________________________\n70EVS-4\n\nE. Answer the following questions :\n1. What are different causes of water pollution ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What are the effects of water pollution ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What is rainwater harvesting ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. How much water is available on Earth ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Visit a water-treatment plant. Observe the different steps of water purification.\nB. Make a list of different types of water filters available in the market. Note their\nspecial features. Find out which is the most popular filter and why ?\nC. Write a note on ʻConservation of Waterʼ :\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nD. Discuss in the class about the factors causing pollution of these water bodies :\n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n______________________________________________________________________________\n(Communication Skills)\n(Environment & Health)\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Experiential Learning)\n71EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nAll of us need house. We know about its importance in our life. But building a\nhouse is not a simple work. It requires a lot of money, material and people.\nLand is the basic requirement for making a house.\nThere is a long list of materials that are required in making of a house. We need\nsand, cement, bricks, steel, stone, wood, glass, tiles, pipes, etc.\nMaking of Bricks\nRead these steps to know how bricks are made :\nStep 1 : First, the fine clay is dug.\nStep 2 : The clay is, then, mixed with water and\nkneaded till it becomes soft.\nStep 3 : The clay dough is, then, put in moulds and\ndried in the Sun.\nStep 4 : The dried bricks are, then, burnt in the kiln.\nStep 5 : Once, the bricks cool down, they are placed on\nthe pallets for storage.\nConstruction of a House\nMany people who are skilled in different jobs, work together to construct a\nhouse or a building.\nThe Architect\nThe is the first one who makes the design of the house or building on architect\na paper. It is called a . This plan shows the layout of the building.plan\n11 Houses and\nBridges\n• Making of Bricks • Construction of a House\n• Bridges\n72EVS-4',1),(550,17,'Shelter','Unit 8 Shelter\n\nThe Carpenter\nThe completes the wood work of the house. carpenter\nHe makes doors, windows, cupboards and furniture\nof the house. He uses tools like saw, gimlet, chisel,\nsquares for his work. He takes the help of his helpers\nto hold the wooden pieces while working.\nThe Plumber\nThe lays the water pipes and taps in the house. plumber\nHe lays the pots, sinks, wash basins, taps and pipes in the\nbathrooms and kitchen. He uses pliers, screw drivers,\nwrench, spanner, chisel, rubber fork cup, etc., for working.\nThe Electrician\nThe installs and maintains the electrical electrician\nconnections of the house. He fits electrical mains,\nelectrical points, plugs, switches safely in the\nbuilding. His main tools are screw drivers, pliers, drill,\nsoldering iron, insulation tape, etc.\nThe Mason\nThe lays the bricks to make walls, roofs and mason\npillars of the houses. He uses tools like trowel,\nhammer, mallet, plumb bot, etc., to make straight walls\nand pillars. Many labourers carry cement and bricks on\ntheir heads and bring it to the mason for work. Mason\nPainter\nThe Painter\nThe paints the building after all the other work are painter\ndone. He uses a ladder.\nThere are many other people who make things in their\nworkshops and deliver them at the construction site.\nThe makes the grills.welder\n73EVS-4\n\nSuspension Bridge\nBeam Bridge\nArch Bridge\nArch Bridge\nArch bridge looks like a semi-circle. It is a very\nstrong bridge made of semi-circular arches.\nEarlier, it was made of stones but now steel,\nconcrete and bricks are used.\nBridges\nThere are many types of bridges. Let’s discuss about them.\nSuspension Bridge\nSuspension bridge has enormous towers of steel\nand concrete set into their foundation. Chains or\nropes hang between the towers. are Footbridges\nsmall suspension bridges made from wood or\nrope which hang over small streams.\nBamboo Bridge\nBamboo bridges are commonly found in villages.\nCantilever Bridge\nA is a modified form cantilever bridge\nof the beam bridge. This type of bridge\nhas support only on one end.\nBeam Bridge\nA is the oldest type of bridge. It beam bridge\nconsist of a horizontal structure called a\nbeam which rests on support on either end.\nThe modern beam bridges are built of steel\nand concrete. A long beam bridge has many\npillars to support the beam.\n74EVS-4\nThe great pyramid of Giza was the tallest structures in the world for over 3800 years.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Why bridges are constructed ?\n2. Which is the most famous bridge\nof India ?\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. is the basic requirement for construction of a house.___________\na) Air b) Water c) Land d) Brick\n2. Construction of a house begin with .___________\na) a building plan b) approval from the government\nc) bricks d) concrete\n3. The electrical connections in the house are fixed by .___________\na) carpenter b) plumber\nc) electrician d) sweeper\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. A lays bricks to make the wall._________________\n2. The design of a building is created by an ._________________\n3. The dried bricks are burnt in a . _________________\n4. The helps the mason._________________\nC. Answer the following questions :\n1. How is a brick made ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. Name the different materials required in making a house.\n____________________________________________________________________\n75EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD For making a house lot of money, labour and material is required.\nD The basic material used to make houses, buildings and bridges is brick.\nD Bricks are made of clay.\nD Ancient houses were made of mud having thatched roofs.\nD Many people who are skilled in different jobs work together to\nconstruct a house or a building.\nD There are many types of bridges—suspension bridge, bamboo bridge,\ncantilever bridge, beam bridge and arch bridge.\n\n3. What is a cantilever bridge ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. Why do bridges need to be strong ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Make a chart on different types of bridges. Hang it in your classroom.\nB. Make a bridge with sticks over a shoe box.\nC. Effect of colour on room temperature :\nHow does the colour of your house affect the temperature inside ? To answer this\nquestion, let us do an experiment :\n1. Carefully cut all three construction paper into pieces so that it can cover the\nentire surface of one of the cardboard boxes.\n2. Use the glue to attach the paper to the box. It should be done as smoothly as\npossible so that there are no air pockets between the paper and the box\nbelow it.\n3. You should now have a white box, a grey box and a black box.\n4. Put the thermometer in the each box, close it and take it outside.\n5. Place the boxes in the sunny spot you picked. Leave them there for 30\nminutes.\n6. After 30 minutes, open the box and quickly note down the temperature on all\nthe thermometers.\nTip : You can try the same procedure in the shade also, still outside and note down\nthe difference in temperature.\nWhich box was the hottest after 30 minutes and which was the coolest ?\nResult : The white box reflects more light than the darker boxes. Therefore, it will\nshow the lowest temperature. The grey box will have higher temperature and the\nblack box will be warmest as it absorbs most of the energy.\nWhy is it necessary to have a proper ventilation system in a building ?\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Art Integration)\n(Creativity)\n(Critical Thinking)\n76EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nIn order to protect themselves from harsh weather and animals, early man lived\nin caves and on high trees. When they started a settled life, they started making\nhouses with muds, stones and straws. It used to be a single room house.\nGradually, separate space for storage of grains, for cooking and for bathing were\nadded. That was the beginning for the modern multi-room houses.\nKutcha House\nOne day, Neeta went to her grandfather’s village\nto attend the wedding of her cousin.\nHer grandfather said, “Neeta, did you notice that\nthe houses in the village are totally different\nfrom the houses in the cities ?”\n“Yes, Grandpa! But why is it so ?” asked Neeta.\nGrandfather replied, “This is because the houses\nin the village are mostly kutcha houses whereas\nin cities there are pucca houses.” Neeta had\nnever heard about kutcha and pucca houses\nearlier. So, she asked her grandfather to explain\nthe differences between these two houses.\nHouses in Rural Areas\nHouses in villages are very simple. They are\ncalled . These are temporary kutcha houses\nhouses made up of mud having thatched roof.\n12 House\nTransformation\n• Houses in Rural Areas • Houses in Urban Areas\n• Slums • Houses—Then and Now\n77EVS-4\n\nThe floor of such houses is smeared with a mixture of cow\ndung and mud. People can collect mud, stones,\nleaves, bamboo, etc., from their surroundings\nwithout spending any money.\nNow-a-days, some houses in rural areas are\nconstructed with better materials like bricks\nand cement. These houses can withstand\nstrong winds and rain. These are called\npucca houses.\nRich people in villages live in big pucca\nhouses known as . They are made havelis\nup of bricks, stones, clay, tiles and thick,\nheavy floors. The roofs are supported\nwith strong wooden beams. Unlike huts,\nthey have many big spacious rooms.\nThere is an or in the aangan courtyard\nmiddle of the haveli.\nHaveli\nPucca House\nHouses in Urban Areas\nAll the houses in urban areas are pucca\nhouses. These are made up of bricks,\niron and steel, cement, tiles, glass and\nmetals. They are very durable and\nstrong houses.\nMulti-Storey Buildings\nNow-a-days, cities are occupied by\nmany multi-storey buildings. These are\ntall buildings with a number of floors.\nEach floor has a number of flats.\nMulti-Storey Building\n78EVS-4\n\nSlums\nMany people who cannot afford to buy or\nbuild houses for themselves, live in these\nslums. A is a cluster of huts built close slum\nto one another in a city because of shortage\nof space. They make kutcha houses with\ncheap and readily available materials.\nSometimes, they even use waste materials\nto make their huts. Slums are totally\nunplanned, unsafe and unhygienic.\nHouses—Then and Now\nIn earlier times, bricks, mud and wood were\nthe common materials used to build a house. Lantern and earthen lamps were\nused for lighting. Very few people had electric connections in their houses. The\nhouses had high roofs and plenty of doors and windows for ventilations. There\nused to be an open space in the centre of the house, where family members\nand guests used to sit on the cots. Sewer\npipes were made of clay. They led from\nbathrooms to sewers and drained into the\nnearby rivers and streams. It was generally a\njoint family system and all the women of the\nhouse used to work together in one kitchen.\nThe breaking down trend of joint families to\nnuclear families brought changes in houses.\nNow builders are also making small houses\nto cater to the requirements of smaller\nnuclear families. The types of houses being\nbuilt today depend on the region and\nclimate of the area.\nSlums\nHaveli\nFlats\n79EVS-4\nDharavi in Mumbai is one of the\nlargest slums in Asia.\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. All houses found in cities are houses.____________\na) kutcha b) pucca c) temporary d) tent\n2. The type of the houses built also depend on the of the area.____________\na) weather b) looks\nc) climate d) None of these\n3. Where are havelis found ?\na) Cities b) Towns c) Villages d) All of these\n4. is breaking down the trend of joint families. ___________\na) Big family b) Community\nc) Nuclear family d) All of these\nC. Match the following :\n1. Pucca houses a) Flat\n2. Kutcha houses b) Ancient house\n3. Slum c) Bricks and concrete\n4. Multi-storey building d) Cluster of huts\n5. Haveli e) Mud\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. and were the first shelter for early man.____________ ____________\n2. As most of the families are nuclear now, houses are also becoming .________\n3. and were used for lighting. ____________ ____________\n80EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Houses in village are simple.\nD Kutcha houses are made up of mud and often need to be repaired.\nD Pucca houses are strong houses made up of bricks, cement, iron and\nsteel, glass, wood, metals, etc.\nD A slum is a cluster of huts built close to one another in a city because\nof shortage of space.\n\n4. Cities are occupied by many buildings.____________\n5. Sewer pipes were made of .____________\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. How men started making houses ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n2. What is a pucca house ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What are different types of houses in cities ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. Describe a slum.\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. With the help of your teacher, organize a visit to a slum area. Talk to the people\nliving there and find out the answers of the following questions :\n1. What materials have you used to make your houses ?\n2. Where do you get drinking water from ?\n3. Do the children living here go to school ?\n4. How do you get electricity ?\n5. What kind of house do you wish for yourself ?\nB. Visit a construction site. Observe the different materials used there. Select\nsamples of these materials and put them in transparent bags. Bring them to the\nclass and show these materials to your classmates.\nC. Write a poem on your house here.\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\n________________________________________________________________________\nMany people in India are poor and homeless. Does giving them some money help to\nsolve their problem ? (Critical Thinking)\n(Multidisciplinary Learning)\n(Experiential Learning)\n(Creativity)\n81EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nRaj wants to visit Habeeb’s house to play cricket. Habeeb kept on explaining him\nthe location of his house but Raj could not understand. Then, Habeeb made a\nmap of his neighbourhood with , showing various , which symbols landmarks\neasily guided Raj to his place. This is the map of Habeeb’s neighbourhood.\nSchool\nHospital\nPost Office\nHouses\nBus Stop\nPark\nSymbols\n+\nBUS\nPark\n+\n13 Mapping\nNeighbourhood\n• Maps • Scale\n• Directions • Compass\n82EVS-4',1),(551,18,'Travel and Communication','Unit 9 Travel and Communication\n\nHabeeb explained to Raj, “From the school go straight to the hospital and turn\nright. You will see a post office there, on the opposite side of the bus stand.\nKeep walking till you see a group of houses on your right. The road ends there.\nTake a right turn from here till you reach a park. Here, you will see another\ngroup of houses on your left. Mine is the second house from the corner.”\nMaps\nA is a drawing of any place—a map\nbuilding, a colony, a village, a town, a\ncountry or world. Map are useful to find\nthe location of a place. A map is much\nsmaller than the size of the actual area of the place which it depicts. A map is\nmade according to a . It is the ratio of the distance between two places as scale\ndrawn on the map and the actual distance between them on the ground. A\nbook of map is called an .atlas\nA map which shows the different states of countries is called a . A political map\nmap which tells us about the physical features of a place is called a .physical map\nDifferent signs and symbols are used on the maps to show various features like\nroads, towns, cities, bridges, streams, forests, dams, wells, post office, etc.\nSymbols are drawn in different colours to show the different landforms and water\nbodies.\nSome symbols are given in the table below :\nInternational\nBoundary\nState\nBoundary\nDistrict\nBoundary\nMetalled Road\nSea Route\nMasjid\nChurch\nTemple\nDam Grass and\nShrub\nA person who studies or makes maps\nis called a cartographer and map\nmaking is called cartography.\n83EVS-4\n\nSigns and symbols used on a map\nRiver or\nStream\nTown or\nVillage\nWell and\nTank\nCapital of\nthe Country\nRailway\nBroad Gauge\nRailway\nNarrow Gauge\nScale\nMaps represent a large area of land in a small drawing, so they use a scale. Map\nscale is the relationship between distance on a map and actual distances on the\nground.\nFor instance, if the distance between two cities is 200 km, we can show it on a\nmap as 2 cm.\nIf, 200 km = 2 cm.\nThen, our scale is 100 km = 1 cm.\nScales can vary from map to map. A map always contains a scale that is uses.\nDirections\nFour major directions are marked on a map. These are— , , andNorth South East\nWest. The top of the map shows the North and the bottom of the map shows\nthe South. East is to the right side of the map and West is to the left side of the\nmap.\nThe four sub-directions are andnorth-east, north-west, south-east\nsouth-west.\nBridge Forest\n84EVS-4\nRAPIDTASK\n1. How Habeeb made it simple to understand for Raj to reach his house ?\n2. Why can’t we use the actual size map ?\n\nCompass\nCompass is an instrument that helps\nus to know the directions. It has a\npointer which always points in the\nNorth direction.\nSouth (S)\nWest (W) East (E)\nNorth (N)\nNorth-West\n(NW)\nNorth-East\n(NE)\nSouth-West\n(SW)\nSouth-East\n(SE)\nMajor and Sub-directions\nTick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. An atlas is a book of ._____________\na) maps b) maths\nc) symbols d) directions\n2. Which of these can be shown as a symbol in a map ?\na) River b) Road\nc) Building d) All of these\n3. Which direction lies between East and South ?\na) north-east b) south-west\nc) south-east d) north-west\n85EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD A map has colours, directions, scale and symbols to represent the\ndetails of a place.\nD Maps represent a large area of land in small drawing, so they use a\nscale.\nD The four major directions are North, South, East, and West.\nD The four sub-directions are north-east, north-west, south-east and\nsouth-west.\nD Compass is an instrument that helps us to know the direction.\n\n4. are used on the maps to show road, bridge, town, etc._____________\na) Atlas b) Symbols\nc) Direction d) Scale\n86EVS-4\nTASKS\nA. Look at this map and answer the questions that follow :\n1. Which place is between the School and the Hospital ?\n_____________________________________________________________________\n2. What is towards the right of the Houses ? _______________________________\n3. Where do we reach if we cross the Raman Road from the Park ?\n_____________________________________________________________________\n4. Describe the location of the Park.\n_____________________________________________________________________\n_____________________________________________________________________\n5. Reach Market from School using two different ways in the map above.\nNehru Road\nRaman Road\nVijay Road\n(Critical Thinking)\nB. Write the meaning of the following colours used on a maps :\n1. 2.\n3. 4.\nPurple\nGreen\nBrown\nRed\n(Conceptual Learning)\n\nC. Take a political map of India with neighbouring countries. Now write the\ndirection of each neighbouring country on the map.\nD. Draw the map of your neighbourhood in your scrapbook. Mark and name the\nimportant landmarks on the map.\nE. A compass is a tool for finding direction. A simple compass is a magnetic needle\nmounted on a pivot, or short pin. We can make it at home also :\nD Cut out a large cardboard circle that is about an inch wider on each side than\nthe mouth of the cup.\nD Cut a smaller circle from the center of the large circle. The smaller circle\nshould be just a bit smaller than the mouth of the cup.\nD Divide the remaining doughnut into four equal quadrants, and label the\nquadrants with the letters N, S, E and W.\nD Stroke a needle with a magnet from eye to tip 30 times. Make sure to stroke it\nin the same direction each time.\nD Cut a cork lengthwise down the middle. Lay the cork so that its new flat (cut)\nsurface faces upwards.\nD Tape the needle to the flat surface, facing it in the same direction as the cork.\nD Fill the cup with water, almost to the rim.\nD Place the doughnut cutout over the mouth\nof the cup, so that the hole is directly over\nthe water.\nD Place the cork on the waterʼs surface so\nthat the needle is pointing from North to\nSouth.\nD Try out your compass, and compare it\nwith the results of a real compass.\n(Art Integration)\n(Multidisciplinary Learning)\n(Knowledge of India)\n87EVS-4\n\nEarly men used to travel on foot and animals. Later they invented wheels that\nhelped them travel to longer distances in shorter time. The people who lived\nnear rivers and sea coasts, developed rafts and boats.\nGoing forward, with the development in the field of science and technology\nmany advanced means of travel were developed. And today, we can cover\nthousands of kilometres in few hours.\nPrivate Means of Transport\nVehicles like cars, scooters, mopeds, motorcycle, etc., which are owned by us\nare called . We do not have to buy tickets for travelling private means of transport\nby a vehicle owned by us. We only have to fill fuel in it and we can go anywhere.\nMotorcycle Car Scooter\nPublic Means of Transport\nVehicles like bus, train, taxi, auto-rickshaw, ships and aeroplanes which are not\nowned by us are known as . We cannot use them as public means of transport\nand when we want. We have to buy tickets or pay some money for travelling by\nthem. Public means of transport are most useful for travelling to far off places.\nFor travelling by public transport, we have to buy tickets. We have to spend\nmoney to buy tickets.\n14 Currency and\nTransport\n• Means of Transport Private and Public • Currency\n• Travelling by Train, Metro and Aeroplane\n88EVS-4\n\nCurrency\nEvery country has a particular type of money that is used to buy things in that\ncountry. It is called the of that country. The currency of India is currency Indian\nRupees (`). The picture of ‘Gandhiji’ is printed on all the currency notes of India.\nIt is printed as a sign of respect to the ‘Father of the Nation’. Currencies made by\nmetals are called and those made of paper coins\nare called . notes\nEach coin has two sides—one side has its value\nmarked on it and the other side has the National\nEmblem. Some coins even have its value and the\nNational Emblem marked on the same side.\nCoins of Different\nDenominations\nTravelling by Train\nFor travelling by train, we have to buy tickets from the ticket counter at the\nrailway station. If we have to travel a long distance, then we should reserve a\nberth in advance. Long distance trains have many coaches like first class AC, 3rd\nclass AC, sleeper, general, etc.\nTicket counter Ticket\n89EVS-4\nCollection and study of coins of different countries is called numismatics.\nRAPIDTASK\n1. Why do we pay for travel ?\n2. How can we identify Indian currency ?\n\nTravelling by Aeroplane\nWe need a passport if we want to visit a foreign country.\nPassport is a document given by the Government of India. The foreign visit is\nalso approved by the Government of India. This is known as .Visa\nWe can travel within India by\nair without a passport. Air\ntravel is an expensive means\nof travel.\nPeople may have to travel\nfrom one place to another\nfor various reasons. They\nmay buy goods from one place and sell it in another\nplace or they may go in search of work or even they\nmight move to a place that has more work opportunities.\nEarlier, travelling was much slower because proper roads were not built. Today,\nwe can travel very fast because of proper road, rail and air transport.\nE-Tickets\nIndian Railway’s Reservation website allows the passengers to buy tickets\nonline and pay by credit or debit cards. Passengers can also check train fares,\ntrain routes, availability, reservation and confirmation of tickets on this site.\nE-tickiting saves us from standing in a long queue for tickets.\nTravelling by Metro Train\nThousands of people travel by Metro Train\neveryday. We can buy for travelling to tokens\nany distance. Regular passengers use smart\ncards. They put the card at the sensing\ndevice on the entry and exit gates and the\nfare is deducted from the card’s account. Metro Train\nAeroplane\nPassport\n90EVS-4\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3\n1. Early man travelled on foot and on ._______________\na) vehicles b) animals\nc) birds d) none of these\n2. Long distance trains have in them._______________\na) boxes b) coaches c) boggies d) chambers\n3. We need tickets for travelling by means of transport. ______________\na) public b) private c) school d) family\n4. It is a private means of transport.\na) Car b) Scooter c) Cycle d) All of these\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. Bus, train, taxi, ships, aeroplanes, all are examples of . _________________\n2. Coins are made of ._________________\n3. Every country has its own . _________________\n4. We can travel by Metro using or ._________________ _________________\n5. Air travel is an means of travel._________________\nC. Answer the following questions :\n1. What brought about a big change in travelling and how ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n91EVS-4\nTTT\nHHH\nUUU\nMMM\nNNN\nAAA\nIII\nLLL\nBBB\nD Travel and transports was revolutionzed by the invention of wheel.\nD Today, we have many different and advanced means of travel.\nD For travelling by public transport, we have to buy tickets.\nD The Indian currency consists of coins and notes.\nD E-ticketing saves us from standing in a long queue for tickets.\nD Air travel is an expensive means of travel.\nD Passport is a document given by the Government of India.\nD Thousands of people travel by metro train everyday.\n\n2. What is the difference between public and private transport ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n3. What is the difference between a coin and a note ?\n____________________________________________________________________\n4. What is the national emblem of India ?\n____________________________________________________________________\nA. Discuss about the usage of fake notes and its effect.\nB. Find the names of the currencies of the following countries and write their\nnames :\nCountry Currency\n1. Bangladesh ____________________________\n2. Bhutan ____________________________\n3. Sri Lanka ____________________________\n4. Pakistan ____________________________\n5. USA ____________________________\n6. UK ____________________________\nC. Draw a picture of any public transport here :\nWhat do you mean by plastic money ? How it is good or bad for us ? (Critical Thinking)\n(Art Integration)\n(Communication Skills)\n(Critical Thinking)\n92EVS-4\nTASKS\n\nA. Tick ( ) the correct option :3 5\n1. A group consisting of parents and their children living together as a unit is called a :\na) Family b) Team\nc) Class d) Staff\n2. A potter makes pots for us._________\na) iron b) copper\nc) wooden d) silver\n3. Birds have in place of their hands._________\na) feathers b) wings\nc) beak d) tongue\n4. A group of elephants is called :\na) Society b) Herd\nc) Locality d) None of these\n5. is the kitchen of a plant._________\na) Root b) Stem\nc) Leaf d) Fruit\nB. Fill in the blanks : 5\n1. A group of people related by marriage, birth and adoption is called a .___________\n2. Birds body is made up of three parts head, trunk and ._____________\n3. An has big external ears._____________\n4. We play games to our mind._____________\n5. The saliva of animals contains ._____________\nC. Write or : True False 5\n1. A nuclear family is also known as extended family.\n2. We need special education to become a professional.\n3. Crow and pigeon fly at great heights.\n4. The colourful petals attract insects like butterflies, etc.\n5. Flowers grow from the fruits.\nTime : 1 hr. M.M. : 40(Based on Chapters–1 to 7) 1\n93EVS-4\n\nD. Match the following : 5\n1. Ostrich a) Scratching bird\n2. Sparrow b) Bird of prey\n3. Hen c) Perching bird\n4. Crane d) Flightless bird\n5. Hawk e) Wading bird\nE. Name the following : 5\n1. Part of the plant that grows above ground. ____________